\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Rand's distress prompts new Fed shock. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 1998\par 18:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 26 (Reuters) - U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ambushed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders for the second time in as many weeks on Friday, the shock value all the greater because it was conducted on behalf of South Africa and the slumping rand. \par \par \uc2  Sales of dollars for rands by the Federal Reserve and the Bank of England on behalf of South Africa temporarily distracted traders from concern about Japan's economy and financial system. \par \par \uc2  However, the ultimate source of rand weakness, as well as turmoil in Russian financial markets which prompted an increase of 20 percentage points in key Russian interest rates, lies in Asia with the yen, analysts and traders said. \par \par \uc2  "We have got \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the rand and rate hikes in Russia, but the cause of the all the instability is still Japan, and we need to see global co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  on this issue," said Claudio Piron, treasury economist at Standard Chartered in London. \par \par \uc2  Speculative attack has driven the rand down almost eight percent against the dollar in the past two days, forcing the South African Reserve Bank to increase its key money-market rate despite the weakness of the economy. But this has only served to heighten pessimism on the rand. \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  temporarily lifted the rand, it soon fell back to new lows and was seen sliding further as traders saw the move depleting South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  "People are not being blinded by the thought of collecting carry on the rand and now the 6.0 level is in their minds," said Simon Drabble, \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  salesman at Standard Bank in London. "But the rand is not an isolated story and the emerging market complex as a whole is looking to become weaker." \par \par \uc2  The rand hit a record low of 5.9150 per dollar despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with a bid rate of 5.5825 at the close of European trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The renewed concern about emerging markets triggered by persistent rand weakness and Russia's rate rise propelled the dollar to two-week peaks against the mark. It also hoisted it to its highest against the yen since the Federal Reserve and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yen's support on June 17. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 1.8135/38 marks at 1606 GMT from 1.7950/60 late in Europe on Thursday. It was at 142.40/50 yen from 141.56/66 yesterday. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc, a traditional safe haven in times of international turmoil, also profited. It appreciated to 84.12/15 centimes per mark from Thursday's one-month low of 84.34. \par \par \uc2  The dollar and the Swiss franc are seen continuing to benefit in the coming weeks as the yen is expected to extend its slide and keep emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia and elsewhere under pressure. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen won't remain on the backburner for very long given that it is the only real game in town," said Mark Cliffe, chief international economist at HSBC Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  He said Japan's quarterly tankan survey of business sentiment out on Sunday at 2350 GMT risked heightening gloom about the outlook for the Japanese economy and could be the next trigger for renewed yen weakness. \par \par \uc2  Fear that the U.S. and Japan could mount another coordinated defence of the yen has so far kept dollar bulls at bay. \par \par \uc2  However, such wariness will eventually evaporate, leaving the yen defenceless if Japan does not act to foster growth and improve confidence in its financial sector, analysts said. This view was reinforced by comments made on Friday by U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin. \par \par \uc2  He said America continued to monitor closely the timing of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but also said the key was for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take action with respect to its economy and particularly its banking system. \par \par \uc2  Merger talks between Sumitomo Trust & Banking Co Ltd and Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan (LTCB), revealed on Friday, did little to assuage traders' concerns about how Japan will deal with the bad loans on its financial institutions books. \par \par \uc2  "If Japan does not do something to get bad loans off balance sheets, the risk is dollar/yen could go back up to 145," said Nick Stamekovic, chief European economist at Bank Austria Creditanstalt Futures in London. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, a quarter-percentage point in Norwegian interest rates proved unsuccessful at lifting the crown which continues to suffer from the weakness in oil prices, Norway's biggest export. \par \par \uc2  The crown was at 4.2430/50 per mark at 1640 GMT from 4.2310/40 late in Europe on Thursday, and only 0.3 percent stronger than the seven-month lows it hit at the end of May. ((Swaha Pattanaik, London newsroom, +44 171 542 6284, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6q01zmx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
