\par\par \b \uc2  Intractable Bad-Loan Problem Deepens Japanese Banks' Borrowing Costs \b0\par\par\uc2 735 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 1998\par 18:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The "Japan premium" - the additional interest rate charged on Japanese banks borrowing dollars - is beginning to rise again amid smoldering fears about the Japanese financial system, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported in its Saturday morning edition. \par \par \uc2  Foreign financial institutions remain wary about the health of Japanese banks, especially their high levels of bad loans, and the recent downgrading of Japanese banks by foreign credit agencies has heightened concerns, the newspaper reported. \par \par \uc2  The three-month Eurodollar Tokyo Interbank Offered Rate (TIBOR) - a gauge of the interest rate on dollar-denominated loans raised by Japanese banks - climbed to 6.05% Friday, up 0.09 percentage point from Thursday and the highest level since early March. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the London Interbank Offered Rate has remained stable at 5.68% recently. \par \par \uc2  Japanese banks unable to procure dollars at low cost are raising yen in the domestic market and exchanging the funds into dollars, before sending the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  overseas. This drove the three-month Euroyen rate up to 0.8-0.9% Friday, the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the bad news from Japan's banking sector keeps coming. In its Saturday morning edition, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported that the combined pretax loss of 146 Japanese banks stood at about 5.26 trillion yen for the fiscal year ended on March 31, according to the Federation of Bankers Associations of Japan. Drops in lending rates caused the banks' net business profit to fall, and the disposal of massive nonperforming loans also took its toll. \par \par \uc2  Japan's nonperforming-loan problem - and the rising "Japan premium" - has been aggravated by a split between the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its powerful Finance Ministry over how aggressively the banks should disclose their problem loans. How the dispute is settled may determine whether Tokyo can stick to some of its recent promises to Washington on cleaning up the bad loans and reversing an economic downturn that threatens to plunge the Asian region into a depper financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, newly independent from the Finance Ministry after a charter revision, has recently begun urging banks to disclose more about their bad-loan portfolios than is required by law, The Wall Street Journal reported recently. But across Tokyo at the Finance Ministry, officials suggest that Japan's banks are already disclosing enough. \par \par \uc2  The rift apparently goes clear to the top. Bank of Japan governor Masaru Hayami has become a vocal supporter of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive line, saying recently that only "voluntary disclosure" of bad loans will provide "clear prospects for re-establishing market confidence and revitalizing the financial system." \par \par \uc2  In contrast, Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga said last week that Japan's banks are already required to meet the same standard of disclosure that the Securities and Exchange Commission requires of American lenders. "The SEC standard is, in fact, the global standard," Matsunaga said. \par \par \uc2  At issue is just which categories of loans a bank should report. Japanese banks, whose known problem loans total nearly $600 billion, now disclose only the worst of their bad loans, those to bankrupt institutions and those whose repayments are considered unlikely. Now, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is calling on the banks to disclose loans that "require monitoring," a wide category that includes loans to everything from the small neighborhood factories having a bad year to the construction companies deep in the red amid the country's prolonged economic downturn. \par \par \uc2  The split appears to be part of a widening philosophical difference between the regulatory agencies. The Bank of Japan has become decidedly more activist since its independence in pushing for changes. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees fuller disclosure as key to restoring this country's financial image. \par \par \uc2  That is also in line with what Washington wants of Japan. The yen has been hammered in recent weeks by worries over the banking crisis. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying yen for dollars in an effort to support the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But U.S. officials say only more disclosure, followed by disposal of bad assets, will lead to a lasting recovery for the yen. Japanese bankers say more disclosure could force them to rein in lending, exacerbating a credit crunch that is already wreaking acting as a brake on economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du6q00xk1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
