\par\par \b \uc2  Data Show U.S. Spent Up To $850 Million In Dollar-Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlene Lee, Staff Reporter \par \uc2 1,035 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 1998\par 05:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The latest data from the Federal Reserve Bank of New York indicate that the Fed and U.S. Treasury spent $800 million to $850 million on last week's joint effort with Japan to prop up the ailing yen, according to analysts. \par \par \uc2  However, it is unclear at this stage what the combined exercise amounted to because the data don't readily give an idea how much \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's share of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was. However, observers believe the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent far more than U.S. monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  In the Fed's latest statement on factors affecting reserve balances for the week ended June 24, "other assets" - which would include foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - increased by $1.049 billion on a daily average basis. Because the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  involved the selling of dollars and the buying of yen, foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves would rise, which in turn would have a positive impact on the net change of the "other assets" column. \par \par \uc2  At the weekly news briefing Thursday, New York Fed spokesmen were quick to stress that the net gain of $1.049 billion in "other assets" largely reflected the premium on securities repurchase agreements carried out during the past week. They declined to provide a breakdown of the "other assets" and the week-to-week changes in these. \par \par \uc2  "You can isolate the premium on RPs," said Lou Crandall, chief economist at R.H. Wrightson & Associates, who closely follows the Fed's reserve data. \par \par \uc2  "The bottom line: the other assets rose about $500 million more than can be explained based on the level of RPs and the ordinary seasonal movement in the category," Crandall noted. \par \par \uc2  That's in the ballpark with the amount the Treasury is believed to have spent - and the Fed and Treasury typically go Dutch whenever the U.S. participates in any \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exercise, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  According to UBS Securities economist Kathy Bostjancic and other analysts, an unexplained extra $417 million showed up last Friday on the U.S. Treasury's daily cash-balance statement. Friday also coincides with the day settlement would have occurred for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was carried out on Wednesday, June 17, settlement in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market takes place two days after a transaction is executed. \par \par \uc2  Of course, what the U.S. spent in the joint-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only part of the picture. The big question remains how much the Bank of Japan spent, and this time the answer isn't easily extrapolated from U.S. data. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, "the BOJ has always \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a larger scale than the U.S.," Crandall said. \par \par \uc2  In April, the Fed sold massive amounts of dollars for yen, but that exercise was entirely on behalf of the BOJ, which left fairly evident tracks on how much it spent in that effort. To finance the April \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ liquidated dollar reserves parked in U.S. Treasury bills, observers noted. A couple of business days after the April 9 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Fed broadsided the T-bill market with the sale of approximately $12 billion in bills on behalf of a "customer" - widely believed, but never confirmed, to be the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  Because the amount was so massive, the Fed couldn't parcel out the order among a few choice financial institutions as it normally does in customer-related T-bill transactions. Instead, it was forced to dispense the $12 billion in bills in a very unorthodox and largely transparent auction-style sale. On top of that, reserve data for the week ended April 15 also showed the Fed itself bought another $6.55 billion in bills outright from a "customer." \par \par \uc2  The total $18.5 billion in T-bills sold to the Fed and the U.S. bill market represent the lion's share of the consensus estimate that the BOJ spent around $20 billion propping up the yen in April. \par \par \uc2  This time around, tallying what the BOJ spent last week isn't as readily discernible. In the latest data, there were no outright transactions between the Fed and a customer during the week ended June 24. The Fed's custody holdings of U.S. government securities on behalf of foreign central banks - a murky figure a best - actually showed an increase of $373 million and not the decline expected when there's been dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  What's more, there was no concrete evidence in the T-bill market of any significant \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling, according to bill traders. Because liquidity has dried up considerably in the T-bill market, it would be hard for players to miss even a discreet sale of a billion or two worth of securities. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, Federal Reserve Bank of New York President William McDonough, who had just accompanied Deputy Treasury Lawrence Summers to Tokyo last week for talks with Japanese and Group of Seven officials, indicated that Japan didn't appear to sell U.S. Treasurys this month as part of the dollar-yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "To the best of our knowledge, there were no Treasurys sold," McDonough told reporters in New York Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  So, how did the BOJ finance its share of the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Some analysts suggested that it may have raised the cash ahead of time. \par \par \uc2  "There was a large reduction in custody holdings the previous week," said Simone Broderick, a Fed and Treasury analyst at Aubrey G. Lanston & Co. The BOJ "could've actually sold the bills beforehand in preparation," she added. \par \par \uc2  On June 17, the day the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place, custody holdings at the Fed were $7.8 billion less than a week earlier, but that figure amounts to merely a clue and not conclusive evidence. Analysts are quick to stress that the figure is a net change that incorporates the collective buying and selling of U.S. Treasurys by foreign central banks during the week. The activity of individual central banks is never broken out. \par \par \uc2  -By Charlene Lee; 201-938-2206; charlene.lee@cor.dowjones.com; with contributions from Brian Blackstone; 201-938-4156 \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  4229 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du6q00x7y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
