\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis/Glance: LTCB-Sumitomo Merger Report Shakes Mkts \b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 1998\par 10:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- The Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan and Sumitomo Trust will merge as early as April, the first-ever merger between a long-term credit bank and a trust bank, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported. LTCB would sell off nonperforming loans to a public bridge bank the government and LDP may form. The report helped boost the Nikkei 0.5% on a late buying surge. \par \par \uc2  Before the merger report, S&P said it would keep LTCB's ratings on watch for possible downgrade, citing the bank's increasingly difficult operating environment and weakened financial profile. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- The dollar rose to Y142.95 in Tokyo, its highest level since coordinated U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  June 17, before falling sharply on profit-taking and the LTCB-Sumitomo report. At 0630 GMT, the dollar was at Y141.46, below Y142.10 in New York Thursday. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Japan EPA Administrative Vice Minister Shioya said the Bank of Japan should consider boosting the amount of money in the financial system. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong interbank rates rose on Financial Secretary Tsang's comments that the government isn't committed to defending the HK dollar peg "at all costs." Tsang reiterated that he stakes his career on the peg's maintenance. [*R/HK] \par \par \uc2  -- A Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said loan problems have been limited to the corporate and trade finance sectors and the territory won't suffer the same meltdown that's afflicted other parts of Asia. [*R/HK] \par \par \uc2  -- People's Bank of China Governor Dai welcomed the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the yen. He told U.S. Treasury Secretary Rubin that Chinese officials were "very happy" with the action. [*R/CH] \par \par \uc2  -- Indonesia's government and the Asia Development Bank signed a $1.5 billion program loan to strengthen and reform Indonesia's financial sector. The 15-year loan will be released in three tranches. [*R/IO] \par \par \uc2  -- The South Korean commerce minister said the country's trade surplus reached $4 billion in June, sharply higher than the $100 million surplus a year ago. [*R/SK] \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore Deputy Prime Minister Lee said a "technical" recession is possible for Singapore, but maintained the economy isn't likely to shrink this year, a Hong Kong newspaper said. [*R/SN] \par \par \uc2  -- Moody's cut the long-term rating for Daiwa Securities units, citing the company's faltering competitiveness in the rapidly deregulating Japanese financial market. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Sluggish domestic demand and declining exports are forcing Japanese steelmakers and petrochemical companies to cut production in the July-September quarter, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- IMF Managing Director Camdessus said he expects no Japanese financial or fiscal reform announcements until at least mid-July, after upper house elections, and then a stream of small actions, rather than a large package. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 26-06-98 \par \par \uc2  0828GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6q05guh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
