\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  fear seen keeping Treasuries on leash. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kathie O'Donnell, \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 1998\par 21:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 25 (Reuters) - Fear that a sharp upside breakout by the dollar may end in a hail of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bullets has U.S. Treasuries biding time near pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, analysts said this week. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar does make another break toward the 145 yen area, then I think we'll see Treasuries begin to make a break again as well," said John Canavan, Treasury market analyst at Stone & McCarthy Research Associates. \par \par \uc2  The September bond contract could retest its 124-14/32 high reached overseas June 16 before last week's joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  halted a dollar rally to an eight-year high, he said. \par \par \uc2  "I certainly think we could go back and retest that level if the dollar begins to hop again without any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Canavan said. "But for right now, I think traders really just sort of want to see what the dollar is going to be able to do, (and) whether or not there will be more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The September bond contract was at 123-7/32 in late trade Thursday. The cash bond was yielding 5.66 percent, just one basis point softer than the benchmark's 5.65 percent closing level the day before the June 17 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Until the market gets more confirmation one way or the other, "I think traders are pretty much on hold," Canavan said. \par \par \uc2  While dollar strength is currently supporting the Treasury market, neither Treasury nor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are willing to "really increase the bid, due to those concerns about the potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the analyst said. \par \par \uc2  Kevin Flanagan, an economist and first vice president at Morgan Stanley Dean Witter, said 142.50 is likely to be a key level for dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  "The feeling in the market right now according to the chartists is 142.50 represents the level that will be tolerated by the (U.S.) Treasury and the Bank of Japan," Flanagan said. "And once you get there, most likely you could bring in some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or at least that will be a test to see if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes." \par \par \uc2  Flanagan said last week's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have been merely a political move to smooth Clinton's China visit and not a "grand scheme" by Japan and the United States to shore up the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Perhaps that's all this was...an attempt to sort of placate the Chinese and not create any type of embarrassment for the president while he is over in China," the economist said. \par \par \uc2  But if the U.S.-Japan move is indeed "a one-shot deal," \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  players will hit the yen hard, Flanagan said. \par \par \uc2  "(Treasuries) are going to continue to track the dollar," he said. "The question is if the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  crowd decides they want to take this dollar up, we are going to follow right along with it, and that will be the key over the next few weeks." \par \par \uc2  A trader at a New York-based primary dealership said fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have tempered the bid for Wednesday's five-year note auction. Players were faced with an issue yielding about 4.5 basis points below the federal funds rate and dollar/yen above 141. \par \par \uc2  But Martin Mitchell, a vice president and manager of institutional government trading at Legg Mason Inc., said any further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely provide a buying opportunity in Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come in and spend money and sell more dollars, but I think it will be a temporary phenomenon. And I think the market views it as that until they really come with any substance as far as fiscal policy over there," Mitchell said. "I look at any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this point as a buying opportunity to the (extent) that the bond market sells off." \par \par \uc2  ((-N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1663)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6p0261k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
