\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends U.S. mixed as Tokyo woes still weigh. \b0\par\par\uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 1998\par 22:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 25 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed against the Japanese yen amid concerns Tokyo may not be ready to tackle economic reforms but slipped against the mark on hopes that foreign aid will help stem Russia's financial woes. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed nearly a full yen higher, managing to cross the psychologically key 142 yen level for the first time since the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined forces and spent billions on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the yen last week. \par \par \uc2  "The reasons for selling yen are the same today as they were yesterday," said Marc Chandler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell. \par \par \uc2  After senior U.S. officials had urged Tokyo to push through new tax cuts, dealers were disappointed when Japan's Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto said recent tax cuts will already help the economy, suggesting these cuts may not become permanent. "Hashimoto's comments were not good for the yen," a dealer said, fearing more such statements as campaigning for Japan's Upper House election on July 12 gets underway. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto also appeared reluctant to force banks to disclose all loans requiring monitoring. This sparked talk that Tokyo may not be ready to abandon its "convoy" system where strong institutions or the government rescues weaker firms. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen closed at 142.05/10 after opening at 141.25/30. Against the mark, the dollar slipped to 1.7975/85 from its opening at 1.7985/88. \par \par \uc2  Trading was particularly nervous as dollar/yen crossed the 142 line and rumours that the Federal Reserve Bank of New York and a large Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were selling dollars helped pushed the U.S. unit briefly lower. \par \par \uc2  "Those rumours are not true, but people were stopped out nonetheless. But then they bought the dollar back up just as quickly and the close suggests we could be heading higher still," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dennis Heidt, chief trader at Paribas, said dollar/yen should "grind higher slowly" rather than jumping up quickly and dealers said the 142.70 level would be critical. "If we get across that, then the way for a retest of the 146.75 high could be seen," a technical analyst said. \par \par \uc2  Expectations for new dollar/yen gains were enhanced when Michael Mussa, research director at the International Monetary Fund, said Japan needs to use public funds to boost consumer lending. \par \par \uc2  Traders did not react to his comments that the yen would rebound if Japan acts on tax plans and stimulus packages. \par \par \uc2  The German mark gained ground amid signs the International Monetary Fund would release a stalled $670 million loan to Russia in order to help keep the economy afloat. The fund announced the payout as New York trading was winding down. \par \par \uc2  Russia's financial crisis, the most severe in recent years, has harmed the mark in particular because of Germany's close relations with Moscow. \par \par \uc2  "The mark nudged higher because people are optimistic the IMF won't let anything bad happen to Russia," Chandler said. \par \par \uc2  There was little market reaction to news Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bundesbank, left interest rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the British pound rose to $1.6712/22 from $1.6650/60. The dollar however climbed against the Swiss franc as that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost its safe-haven lustre amid signs international action was being taken on Russia's crisis. Dollar/Swiss closed at 1.5155/65, up from 1.5083/93. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to Canadian $1.4659/64 from C$1.4674/79. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar ended at $0.6063/68, up from its $0.6037/42 opening level. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6p025p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
