\par\par \b \uc2  Japan politicians vow banking revamp as yen falls. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Kobayashi \par \uc2 737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 1998\par 06:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 24 (Reuters) - Japanese politicians vowed Wednesday to clean up the nation's banking mess, calling for a cut in the number of banks as the yen slid again on a lack of confidence in Tokyo's ability to deal with Japan's economic problems. \par \par \uc2  "What we are aiming to protect is the financial system. We are not thinking of artificially rescuing those (banks) which are bad," Jiji news agency quoted Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto as saying. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto also told the news conference, which was closed to foreign reporters, that competition will ultimately determine the number of Japanese banks, Jiji said. \par \par \uc2  Senior members of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) have called for wholesale changes in the banking sector, which is burdened by overcapacity and excessive competition. \par \par \uc2  "The number of Japanese banks should be halved. So should their lending," senior LDP member and former Cabinet secretary Seiroku Kajiyama said in a speech, explaining that the Japanese economy was overdependent on banks for raising funds. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the politicians' remarks showed that a sense of crisis was finally beginning to take hold. \par \par \uc2  Michio Ochi, who belongs to a joint LDP-government panel discussing the disposal of bad loans, told Reuters Television that while he did not want to see any bank failures, Japan's 19 money centre banks need to be reduced to about 10. \par \par \uc2  Money centre banks play a major national and international economic role because they are large lenders, depositories and buyers of money market instruments and securities. \par \par \uc2  Economists said there were still doubts in the financial markets as to whether drastic steps would be taken soon to address Japan's banking woes. \par \par \uc2  The dollar surged by 2 yen to over 141 yen in Tokyo on Wednesday, breaking above the 140 level for the first time since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  one week ago, on June 17, in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the sagging yen. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar's rise Wednesday was hindered by speculation of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said it might climb as high as 142 yen in the near future due to questions over how Japan would solve its bad loan mess. \par \par \uc2  "We can no longer avoid a situation where two or three banks will be forced to collapse. If we keep on putting a lid on something that stinks, the economy will continue to decay," said Osamu Katano, an economist at Nikko Research Centre Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Economists say Japanese banks have three choices - invite foreign corporate participation, restructure their businesses on their own, or go out of business. \par \par \uc2  "Banks, politicians, the man on the street ... everybody has to share the pain somewhere," said Yasukazu Shimizu, an economist at NCB Research Institute Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Japan has already set aside 30 trillion yen ($212 billion) to help ease worries over its financial system, triggered by a series of financial firm bankruptcies since last November. \par \par \uc2  Ministers and senior LDP officials have insisted the amount was sufficient to deal with current problems, but some have suggested more public funds to set up a bridge bank, which would protect relatively sound borrowers from a credit lapse. \par \par \uc2  "If Japan is able to take steps to stabilise financial system worries once and for all, the pain will be great and the economy will worsen. But that would be the bottom. Japan will be able to get back on a normal recovery track," Shimizu said. \par \par \uc2  Industry data released Wednesday showed Japanese consumers were anaemic spenders in May. Sales figures from department stores, chainstores and automakers barely budged from year-ago levels, dragging along at the depressed pace set after last spring's national consumption tax increase. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday that Japan's top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, has retained his post as vice finance minister for international affairs for another year. There had been speculation that Sakakibara would be replaced in this month's annual ministry personnel shuffle. \par \par \uc2  He was largely credited for masterminding last week's joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara had been criticised by some in the ruling LDP for orchestrating Japan's earlier effort to prop up the yen in April, which proved ineffective despite its massive size - $20 billion. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Japan needed Sakakibara to stay in his post at a time of such volatility in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6o02b3q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
