\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar calm, stg/mark hits 7-wk high. \b0\par\par\uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 1998\par 16:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 24 (Reuters) - Dollar/yen had regained its composure by late Europe after a sharp slide in early trade, with the market awaiting any news regarding Japan's plans for bank reform and U.S. President Bill Clinton's visit to China. \par \par \uc2  The dollar spent the day in a narrow range against the mark, with the German \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movements against a softening yen and the rallying British pound grabbing more attention than the dollar/mark pair. \par \par \uc2  "We've sort of gone on hold now (regarding dollar/yen)...the market is wary of potential news breaking," said Adam Cribben, assistant chief trader Credit Lyonnais in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 140.23/33 yen at 1435 GMT against its late Tuesday European level of 139.19/29. \par \par \uc2  It had moved choppily in a two-yen range, with the market getting a brief scare early on when a strengthening dollar/yen suddenly nosedived amid rumours of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders, however, soon attributed the drop to large bank orders. \par \par \uc2  "There's scepticism about whether Japan can put together a meaningful package (for the banking system) and that's why we've seen yen weakness creeping back in," said Cribben. "If nothing comes out, we'll see dollar/yen go up unless there's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...and that would just be a short-term solution." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policymakers have begun to discuss setting up a "bridge bank" which could lend money to relatively sound borrowers if their creditor banks failed. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said the situation in Japan was a cause of considerable concern and that Tokyo urgently needed to take action on banks. He also said it was crucial to take followup action after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the market was watching Clinton's five-day visit to China which begins on Thursday, in view of Beijing's recent complaints that the yen's weakness was hurting China's economy. \par \par \uc2  Traders and analysts said they would be on the lookout for any comments regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from either the U.S. or Chinese leaders, but added that they expected nothing major. \par \par \uc2  "The market is cautious, but I don't think there's any real ground for it," said Joanne Collins, senior economist at Daiwa. \par \par \uc2  "No doubt they will discuss it (\b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) and China will make noises about the damage the yen is doing to its economy... But there's not much they can do apart from talk." \par \par \uc2  The German mark paid scant attention to news regarding its economy, with traders saying they were looking for developments in the Russian financial crisis for incentive. \par \par \uc2  The IMF's First Deputy Managing Director Stanley Fischer said on Wednesday that Russia had to implement fiscal and other reforms before it could expect additional credit from the IMF, and that discussions on more aid could be lengthy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 1.8010/15 marks after trading in a range of less than one pfennig during the session, against 1.8040/45 in late Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/mark's taken a back seat now and is really a function of the mark crosses...for instance sterling/mark is doing well because of the anticipation of a (UK) rate rise," said Cribben at Credit Lyonnais. \par \par \uc2  The pound shot up to its highest in seven weeks against the mark, with traders citing a breach of technical resistance for today's rise in addition to the recent rate hike speculation. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark had fallen back to 3.0017/27 from its high of 3.0120, but was still above late Tuesday's 2.9989/99. \par \par \uc2  And the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Norway, the world's second-biggest oil exporter, rose against the mark to its highest in two weeks on bullish news for the oil market. OPEC members said they had agreed to cut oil output by a larger amount than expected in a bid to bolster flagging oil prices. The Norwegian crown rose to 4.2090 per mark, up from 4.2150/70 late on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom, +44 171-542-6721, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6o02at4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
