\par\par \b \uc2  WORLD BONDS - Correlation strong as weak Asia drags. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Aline van Duyn \par \uc2 830 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 1998\par 16:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 24 (Reuters) - Bond market movements have become more strongly correlated in recent months as the Asian crisis extends its global grip, leaving little room for outperformance between different sectors. \par \par \uc2  The dollar/yen exchange rate is the most immediate barometer of twists and turns in sentiment towards Japan and Asia as a whole, and changes there are quickly reflected in other markets. \par \par \uc2  "Whatever position you are putting on - in bonds, equities, credit, emerging markets, curve trades, the Australian dollar - you are trading the Asian outlook," said Jan Loeys, global fixed income strategist at J.P. Morgan in London. \par \par \uc2  "There are no diversification trades, there is no hiding from the Asian crisis," he said. \par \par \uc2  The big phenomenon this year has been a flight to quality, benefiting U.S. and core European government bonds in particular. The recession in Japan and other Asian countries, together with mounting short-term debts and deep-seated structural problems, has led to ever-weaker Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Investors have generally bailed out of all potentially volatile assets and instead increased exposure to "safe-haven" bond markets. \par \par \uc2  "The flight to quality from Asia and Japan has seen more investing in European core markets and the U.S. and this flow of funds is making correlation very high," said Cesar Molinas, co-head of European economic and fixed income research at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  He said there had been expectations that the introduction of the euro would result in less correlation between European bond markets and the U.S., but the opposite had happened. \par \par \uc2  "It's too early to say that the euro is not of sufficient size and importance to make interest rate determination in Europe more independent of the U.S. At the moment it's flight to quality that is driving correlation," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Asian turmoil affects the rest of the world economy because of its deflationary impact. Interest rate expectations have shifted as a result, although yields remain just under 80 basis points higher in the U.S. than in Germany at the 10-year maturity. \par \par \uc2  "If Asia was absolutely stable then U.S. bond yields would be higher than they are today," said John McNeill, market strategist at Sutherlands brokerage in Edinburgh. \par \par \uc2  "People are looking at bond markets today with more than half an eye on Asia, the domestic economy is not the determinant for U.S. bond yields," he said. \par \par \uc2  With dollar/yen the most watched gauge of shifts in sentiment, moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are quickly reflected in bond markets. \par \par \uc2  Loeys at J.P. Morgan said, the price move correlation between asset classes was only an apparent one. \par \par \uc2  "It's not a causal correlation because it is caused by a third factor - which is Asia's economic weakness," Loeys said. \par \par \uc2  "It's not directly dollar/yen which is affecting Treasuries, it's more indirect. It's changes in the perception about whether the Japanese have got their act together in sorting out their economy." \par \par \uc2  Loeys did see some room for small outperformance of Bunds in the coming weeks because of the difference in the shape of the yield curve compared with the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "Bunds have a good chance for outperforming because they give a positive cost of carry," he said. "The 10-year spread could go to 80-85 b.p. in the coming weeks from 78 b.p. at the moment." \par \par \uc2  Molinas at Merrill Lynch said the cross flows of funds between Europe and the U.S. were very weak, with investors sticking to their domestic markets, and this would not pick up again until a turnaround in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The big question for bond traders is when the flight to quality will reverse, which in turn will only happen once Japan is on the road to recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The correlation works for a while but then the relationship will break down and it is difficult to determine when that will happen," said McNeill. \par \par \uc2  "The relationship is a recent phenomenon, it hasn't been there for ever." \par \par \uc2  In the meantime, U.S. Treasury bonds are expected to continue their rally higher, with 30-year benchmark yields hitting record lows last week and on course to reach 5.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen will stay closely watched, especially for signs of renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favour of the yen, but it alone will not signal a change in bond market trends. \par \par \uc2  Loeys said bond markets fell last week after the U.S. Federal Reserve and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the yen because they thought it signalled the U.S. had reassurance Japan was seriously tackling its problems. \par \par \uc2  "It is now clear there is no quick fix to the problems," he said, adding he remained bearish on the outlook for Asian economies. ((London Newsroom +44 171 542 6176 fax +44 171 542 8688, uk.governmentbonds.news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6o02asz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
