\par\par \b \uc2  Asia stocks end mixed, eyes on weak yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chris McCall \par \uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Reuters) - Asian stock markets mostly stood firm Wednesday in the face of a renewed slide in the yen's value to end with mixed fortunes. \par \par \uc2  But traders said they were keeping their eyes firmly on the beleaguered Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which dipped below 141 to the dollar at one point as market confidence in Japan's will to deal with its problems waned. \par \par \uc2  The dollar surged by 2 yen in Tokyo, breaking above the 140 level for the first time since the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  came to the yen's rescue with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. But the yen rebounded in late trade to stand at around 139.8 to the dollar at 6:15 a.m. EDT. \par \par \uc2  A strong close on Wall Street overnight helped boost shares in Tokyo, Taiwan and Sydney, Australia, and Hong Kong was helped up by late yen strength. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the region's overwhelming concern remained the fate of the beleaguered Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which in turn hinged on how quick and bold Tokyo's latest financial reforms turned out to be. \par \par \uc2  "People are very nervous about what's happening in Japan, extremely nervous," said dealer Peter Struk of Reynolds & Co. in Sydney. "In a lot of stocks, buyers have just pulled right out." \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's key Nikkei average ended up 0.46 percent as a strong performance among high-tech stocks helped outweigh the effects of the yen's fall. The Nikkei closed at 15,123.18, a gain of 68.58 points. \par \par \uc2  A record close of New York's Nasdaq 100 index of technology issues boosted Japanese high-tech plays, along with prospects for higher export earnings from the weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  Sony Corp was up 330 yen at 11,380, and Canon Inc. rose 80 to 3,190. TDK Corp gained 160 yen to 10,350 and Advantest was up 390 to 7,330. \par \par \uc2  However, the most actively traded issue was Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan, amid confusion over its future. It fell 1 yen to 70. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty over government plans to rid the banking sector of its crushing bad debts continued to weigh on sentiment. \par \par \uc2  But some dealers said they had been encouraged by recent comments by leading politicians on the establishment of a "bridge bank," seen as a key part of any reform package. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like they're really going to do something substantial about the bad banks," said Michael Wilkins, a dealer at Credit Lyonnais. "The more concrete proposals they come out with, the better." \par \par \uc2  In Hong Kong, the Hang Seng index gained 0.94 percent to 8,296.77, up 77.10 points. \par \par \uc2  Sydney's All Ordinaries index ended up 0.39 percent at 2,592.8. Concerns over Japan made investors prone to take quick profits from early U.S.-led gains, closing 10.2 points higher. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar, which has traded just under US$0.61 in the past few days, eased slightly as the yen came under pressure. \par \par \uc2  The head of one of Japan's four main business groups said in Tokyo the yen could weaken further if steps to tackle the country's banking woes failed to meet overseas expectations. The moves are due to be announced by July 8. \par \par \uc2  "It's a very important issue. If the measures go against global expectations, then the market could move in the wrong direction and the yen could fall," said Jiro Ushio, head of the Japan Association of Corporate Executives (Keizai Doyukai). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6o026v7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
