\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar dips below 140 yen by midday Europe. \b0\par\par\uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 1998\par 12:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 24 (Reuters) - The dollar dipped below 140 yen by the European midsession as a sudden drop from one-week highs in early trading showed the market remained concerned about the strength of the dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "We are back to where we were two or three weeks ago," said Dick Howard, senior economist at Julius Baer Investments. \par \par \uc2  "The market is looking to push the yen lower, but fear of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is stopping it from going too far." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Reserve and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars to support the yen last Wednesday, after weeks of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation. \par \par \uc2  There were fears in early June that the 140 level would trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the central banks did not step in until around 142.20, about one yen above Wednesday's highs. \par \par \uc2  The market showed the extent of its nervousness in early European trade on Wednesday, when a 1-1/2 yen drop in dollar/yen sparked talk of involvement by the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen dropped 150 points in 10 seconds, too fast to trade anything," said a trader from a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  "There were rumours that the Bank of Japan was checking rates, but it wasn't confirmed and I don't believe it," he added. \par \par \uc2  Traders discounted talk that the Bank of Japan and Bank of England were checking dollar/yen rates, and said apparent dollar sales by the Swiss National Bank may have been for the purpose of adjusting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings. \par \par \uc2  A Japanese bank sold a large order of dollars in thin early London trade, causing the drop, they added. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen traded at 139.84/94 at 1018 GMT, from 139.19/29 in late Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Trade will remain anxious ahead of more details from Tokyo on the set-up of a "bridge bank" to lend money to relatively sound borrowers left exposed if their creditor banks failed. \par \par \uc2  A senior official from Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) said it would be impossible to finalise the bridge bank plan before July 8. \par \par \uc2  The markets are also awaiting Japanese Upper House elections on July 12, and, ahead of that, Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey of business sentiment, to be released late on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials Eisuke Sakakibara and Haruhiko Kuroda kept their posts in annual personnel changes announced on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark hovered below 1.80, shrugging off a 0.2 percent rise in preliminary June west German consumer prices. \par \par \uc2  West German May producer prices were unchanged month-on-month and Germany's Ifo institute said it saw indications of a moderate weakening of German export growth. \par \par \uc2  "There is a lot of news which is positive for the dollar against the mark, yet the dollar hasn't moved," said Howard, adding this meant the dollar was vulnerable to the downside. \par \par \uc2  International Monetary Fund deputy head Stanley Fischer said he would recommend the IMF board release the next $670 million tranche of an existing multi-billion dollar loan for Russia. But Fischer told reporters in Moscow a new loan would require lengthy negotiation and was not yet certain. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark traded at 1.7986/96 at 1038 GMT, from 1.8040/45 in late Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders will be watching for news from Federal Reserve Bank of New York President William McDonough, who takes part in a panel discussion in New York at 1200 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, the pound struggled above the 3.00 mark level amid talk of large options-related sell orders, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But concern about Russia and Asia's financial woes kept the pound underpinned, and speculation about higher British interest rates continued to limit the downside. \par \par \uc2  The pound traded at 2.9985/95 marks and $1.6674/84, from 2.9989/99 marks and $1.6624/34 late on Tuesday. ((Carolyn Cohn, London newsroom +44 171 542 6320, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6o026ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
