\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr dips in late Tokyo,but still holds on to gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 1998\par 09:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  0653 GMT\tab     TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0600 GMT\par  USD/JPY     140.33/38\tab \tab 141.02-138.89\par  USD/DEM     1.8000/05\tab \tab 1.8056-1.8005\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2      TOKYO, June 24 (Reuters) - The dollar dipped suddenly \par \par \uc2  against the yen in late Wednesday trade on a huge amount of \par \par \uc2  selling by overseas funds and talk of yen buying by a European \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but still held on to most of the day's gains. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's abrupt fall from 141 to below 140 yen caught \par \par \uc2  many traders here by surprise, which ignited speculation that \par \par \uc2  the Bank of Japan (BOJ) might have sold dollars for the yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders here said they could not find evidence of dollar sales by the BOJ and detected active sales by overseas funds and a Japanese bank instead. \par \par \uc2  There was talk the Swiss National bank (SNB) sold dollars for the yen. Some said the bank's apparent sales might have been for the purpose of adjusting its foreign reserve holdings. \par \par \uc2  "Given the dollar's quick fall, it's no wonder people are assuming that the BOJ sold dollars to prop up the yen," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  "But I believe that the BOJ had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet as there weren't follow-through sales," he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded to around 140.57/67 yen as of 0715 GMT after briefly falling as low as around 139.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 139.20/25 yen and 1.8017/22 marks in New York on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  A senior dealer at a European bank in Tokyo said the dollar buying spree had been cooled by the quick drop. \par \par \uc2  Before giving up some gains in late trade, the greenback had surged by two yen amid growing frustration over slow moves by the Japanese government to tackle its banking sector problem and the fading effects of U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that took place a week earlier, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Traders earlier said that the dollar may rise to 142 yen in the near term and a few even said they expect the dollar to shoot up to 145 yen soon unless the U.S. Federal Reserve steps in to sell dollars later in the day. \par \par \uc2  There was a growing perception that the Japanese government might not meet the expectations of the financial markets for swift and effective solutions to the banks' bad loan problems. \par \par \uc2  Michio Ochi, a senior ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) official, told Reuters there was no conclusion in sight for the government's bad-loan plan and it would be impossible to finalise the plan before July 8. \par \par \uc2  "It is not possible to come up with the conclusion before July 8," Ochi said, adding that there were still many ideas floating among party members. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, senior LDP official Seiroku Kajiyama said in a speech that the number of Japanese banks and amount of their total lending should be halved. \par \par \uc2  But traders were not impressed by their remarks due to the lack of clarity over how to clean up the banking mess. \par \par \uc2  The Japan premium, the extra cost Japanese banks have to pay in the euro interbank markets, widened due to deepening worries over some Japanese banks, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  For three-month euroyen interest rates, the premium was 30 basis points (BPS) over the 0.38 percent European and U.S. banks with better credit ratings were paying. The premium was 24 to 25 BPS on Tuesday and 20 to 21 BPS on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Foreign bank dealers said some Japanese banks with difficulty raising funds in the money markets might be forced to buy dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  At midday, the dollar broke through the key barrier of 140 yen as interbank dealers who had been expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were caught holding dollar shorts, which ignited a flurry of stop-loss dollar buying. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda declined to comment on Wednesday morning about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Vice Finance Minister Koji Tanami, asked to comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, said: "It's the same as before." He did not elaborate. \par \par \uc2  Asked about Fitch IBCA's warning on Tuesday that Japan's sovereign credit rating could be undermined due to its domestic recession, soft equity prices and the weak yen, Tanami told reporters it was important for Japan to pursue stable growth. \par \par \uc2  ((Tatsuo Ito, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6o022wb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
