\par\par \b \uc2  Fed's McDonough Says  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Didn't Sell U.S. Treasurys In  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1998\par 15:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't appear to sell U.S. Treasurys this month as part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the U.S. Federal Reserve to boost the yen, New York Federal Reserve President William McDonough said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "To best of our knowledge, there were no Treasurys sold," he said during a conference here on economic development. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to reporters, McDonough contrasted the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April, when it was "widely known" that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold U.S. Treasurys to help it boost the yen. \par \par \uc2  McDonough also said Japan must seize the current window of opportunity to reform its economy and prevent it from spiraling deeper into recession. \par \par \uc2  "Japan would be very well advised to move in that direction," McDonough said. \par \par \uc2  McDonough traveled to Japan last week with Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers to discuss economic proposals with officials from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  The trip came just ahead of a hastily called weekend meeting of finance officials from the Group of Seven major industrial nations to discuss ways to reform the Japanese economy and prevent its problems from adding further to the current economic weakness throughout Asia. \par \par \uc2  Chief among the G-7 concerns was that yen weakness would force China to devalue the yuan to prevent an erosion of China's trade position, thereby sparking off a new round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unrest in the region. \par \par \uc2  McDonough said he doesn't think China should devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "I believe it is not in China's interest to devalue the yuan," he said. \par \par \uc2  "It's not a question of whether they have to," he said, noting that China has a relatively strong economy which is still growing. \par \par \uc2  Speaking about the U.S. economy, McDonough said it will settle into a 2.5% to 3% range for gross domestic product growth without creating additional inflationary pressures. He didn't specify the time period. \par \par \uc2  A tug of war will continue between weak exports and strong productivity growth, he said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. productivity in the last two and a half years has risen to about 2.5%, versus an average level of 1.0% since 1973. But McDonough noted, without citing figures, that the strong dollar has weakened exports to Asia. \par \par \uc2  Although McDonough wasn't prepared to admit that "a new paradigm" has occurred, "there are reasons to believe we have had a really important improvement," he said. \par \par \uc2  Going forward, McDonough said he sees "solid economic performance" in the U.S., fueled by domestic consumer and construction spending. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  4213 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du6n00ygo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
