\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup -2: Clinton's China Visit May Cap Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1998\par 08:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose against the yen as well in the aftermath of Saturday's meeting, with sterling up against the yen but down against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The mark is quoted at Y76.69, up from Y76.07 in New York late Friday and above Y75.07 in London late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling is at $1.6645, down from $1.6720 in New York late Friday. Sterling was at $1.6686 in London late Friday. \par \par \uc2  About the only thing holding the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back is U.S. President Bill Clinton's visit to China this week. China's veiled threats to devalue the yuan if the U.S. didn't support the weakening yen may have been the deciding factor in the U.S. decision to join with Tokyo in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  Since Clinton doesn't want to be embarrased while in Beijing, the dollar will likely be capped around Y140, although some feel it could get as high as Y142 this week, the level at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks more and more like a political decision, one that (U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert) Rubin opposed," the U.K. bank trader said. "I'm not sure the U.S. can keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there's a policy vacuum from (Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro) Hashimoto." \par \par \uc2  Senior officials at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  or MOF did their best to keep the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market's mind, with vice finance minister for international affairs Eisuke Sakakibara, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market "when we think it's appropriate." \par \par \uc2  But with sentiment mounting that the bad loans plaguing Japan's banking sector must be dealt with as soon as possible, the yen will succumb to selling until concrete policies emerge from MOF and the government. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "What will Hashimoto do to reform the financial system? That's the\par key," Nippon Credit's Shioiri said.\par   -By John Brinsley; 813-5255-2936; E-MAIL jbrinsley@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 22-06-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0604GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6m05b4v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
