\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Bad-Loan Crisis/Rift -2: Insist Bank Still Solvent \b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1998\par 01:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  "Japan's banks don't understand that this is what it will take to restore trust in the banking system," said a regulatory official who declined to be named. "Sometimes to gain trust, you have to do more than what is required." \par \par \uc2  The split appears to be part of a widening philosophical difference between the regulatory agencies. Since its independence, the Bank of Japan has become decidedly more activist in pushing for reforms. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees fuller disclosure as the key to restoring this country's image. \par \par \uc2  That is in line with what Washington wants of Japan. The yen has been hammered in recent weeks by worries over the banking crisis. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying yen for dollars in an effort to support the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But U.S. officials have said only more disclosure, followed by the disposal of bad assets, will lead to a lasting recovery in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Japanese bankers - apparently with the agreement of the Finance Ministry - have said more disclosure could force them to rein in lending, aggravating a credit crunch that is already wreaking havoc on the economy. "Forcing us to disclose more could even cause us to call in loans to even healthy borrowers," said one official at a major Japanese bank, adding, "Where would that leave the economy?" \par \par \uc2  Still, a lack of transparency has caused Japanese banks no end of problems. Investors simply can't tell from the banks' disclosures how bad the problems are. That's one reason international money markets charge Japan's banks a premium on funds they borrow. This "Japan premium" has surfaced repeatedly over the past year and has haunted even those lenders that have been aggressive at writing off problem loans. \par \par \uc2  And markets can be unforgiving when it comes to doubts over the financial health of private institutions. Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan Ltd. on Monday saw its share price fall 50 yen, or 45%, to 62 yen, its lowest level in decades, on fears that its problems are insurmountable. Japanese media reports have said the bank is considering merger options. \par \par \uc2  The bank has so far denied all the merger reports. And regulators insist the bank is still solvent. But under Japan's standards of disclosure, said stock traders, there is no way to be certain. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 22-06-98 \par \par \uc2  2308GMT \par \par \uc2  1121 4276 62542 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6m059vj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
