\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr kicks off firm in Europe after G7 disappoints. \b0\par\par\uc2 524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1998\par 09:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 22 (Reuters) - The dollar was higher in Europe on Monday and was expected to keep firm after officials from the Group of Seven (G7) failed to boost confidence in the yen. \par \par \uc2  G7 and Asian officials made no specific reference to the yen in a statement released after a meeting on Saturday. They said it was vital for Japan, Asia and the world economy that Japan restore the health of its banking system, achieve domestic demand-led growth and open its markets. \par \par \uc2  "We're targeting 142 at the end of this week, based on the lack of specific plans and timetables coming from the G7 meeting," said Jesper Dannesboe at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  Dannesboe, a treasury economist, said dollar gains were likely to be slow though because of the market's concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market will be cautious to take the dollar to high too fast but the economic situation in Japan is likely to worsen before it gets better, so there is room for the yen too weaken." \par \par \uc2  At 0750 GMT the dollar was near European session highs, trading at 138.41/46 yen, just above overnight highs around 138.40 and up over three percent from 134.12/22 in late European trading on Friday. It was also firmer against the mark at 1.7987/92 from 1.7838/48. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had first resistance around 139 yen ahead of the firmer 142 level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  G7 and Asian officials said on Saturday joint U.S. and Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to boost the yen had created a "window of opportunity" for Japan to repair its economy but that the chance would not last forever. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was knocked off highs temporarily overnight after Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when appropriate. He said the G7 nations and other countries were in close contact on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooperation. \par \par \uc2  "The upside of the dollar will be the area to test with each new day without fresh news and without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Tim Fox, chief economist at Standard Chartered in London. \par \par \uc2  The market will focus on news about how Japan will solve its banking bad-loan problem and whether it can find ways to effectively boost its economy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also firm against the mark on speculative mark selling amid concern about the situation in Russia and lingering talk about a possible ruble devaluation, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The mark tends to weaken amid economic trouble in Russia due to German banks' close links with the country. \par \par \uc2  There was little reaction to softer than expected German M3 data for May. M3 rose an annualised 4.4 percent against the fourth quarter of 1997, compared with 4.8 percent forecast and 4.7 percent the previous month. \par \par \uc2  The Bundesbank said M3 growth was dampened by foreign outflows and a decline in public debt. ((Astrid Zweynert, London newsroom, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 542 52 93, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6m02d8e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
