\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Yen slips, Japan stocks rise slightly after G7. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kanta Watanabe \par \uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1998\par 06:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 22 (Reuters) - The dollar gained around two yen in Tokyo on Monday while stocks made modest gains after a weekend meeting of financial officials from G7 and Asian nations failed to boost confidence in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But fears of another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented aggressive buying of dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  In early Asian trade, the dollar rose as high as around 138.90 yen due to short-covering and speculative purchases. It stood about 138.38 yen at 0740 GMT, nearly five yen above Friday's New York low of around 133.60 yen and against a New York close of 136.65/75. \par \par \uc2  Traders said market participants were focusing on what steps Japan would take to resolve the bad loan mess bedeviling its banking sector and whether it can find ways to effectively boost its economy. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks rose as a senior member of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party pledged to complete details of a widely discussed "bridge bank" that would provide loans to sound borrowers of failed financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average closed up 41.11 points, or 0.27 percent, at 15,309.09. September futures were down 10 at 15,270. \par \par \uc2  Koichi Kato, secretary-general of the LDP, publicly committed to completing details for a bridge bank by July 8 - before July 12 elections to Japan's Upper House of parliament. "We have to wait at least two weeks, which makes it difficult (for stocks) to head higher," said Masatoshi Sato, manager at Kankaku Securities Co. \par \par \uc2  Urgency was added to Japan's need to fix the bad loan problem as speculation mounted over a possible merger of ailing Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan Ltd. \par \par \uc2  LTCB shares ended down 50 at 62 yen against Friday's close of 112. LTCB, Japan's second-largest long-term credit bank, denied a report in the Yomiuri Shimbun newspaper that it was thinking of merging with Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank Ltdand Daiwa Bank Ltd. \par \par \uc2  The dollar pared gains earlier in the day due to concerns over possible dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  after comments by top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters Tokyo would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when appropriate, adding that it was in close contact with other Group of Seven and Asian nations on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooperation. \par \par \uc2  Senior Asian and G7 financial officials said in a statement on Saturday they would cooperate on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. They also said it was vital for Japan, Asia and the entire world economy that Tokyo "restore its banking system to health, achieve domestic demand-led growth, (and) open and deregulate its markets". \par \par \uc2  The yen's rally last week, after surprise joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States on Wednesday, came to a halt on Friday after U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin poured cold water on yen bulls by saying the weekend meeting was unlikely to yield new plans for reforming Japan's economy and that such plans would "take time". Elsewhere on Monday, Japanese government bonds (JGBs) were firmer, but the yield curve steepened on the re-emergence of the Japan premium - the extra cost to Japanese banks when raising funds in euro interbank markets - traders said. \par \par \uc2  The yield of the benchmark 182nd 10-year JGB stood at 1.255 percent at the close, down from 1.270 percent on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 9780 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6m02d4w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
