\par\par \b \uc2  HK central banker sees rates staying high - paper. \b0\par\par\uc2 267 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1998\par 03:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, June 22 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) said local interest rates were likely to remain high until confidence returned to the banking systems of the territory and the region, a newspaper reported on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "If the degree of confidence is high, there won't be speculation and all that, and interest rates will come down to levels nearer to U.S. dollar interest rates," HKMA chief executive Joseph Yam told the Hong Kong Standard. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar interest rates currently ranged from five percent to six percent while Hong Kong rates ranged from eight percent to 14 percent, the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  The chief of the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted as saying the stability of the Japanese yen was the key to renewed confidence in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the banking systems in the Asia-Pacific region. \par \par \uc2  He was also quoted as saying there had to be concerted efforts by central banks in the region to prevent the yen from falling to critical levels again after it had stabilised on the back of the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  Yam told the newspaper he expected the local banking industry to come under pressure to consolidate in the light of the severity of the economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA would let market forces determine the pace of consolidation, he was cited as saying. \par \par \uc2  Yam was not immediately available to comment on the report. \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6590; Fax (852) 2845 