\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View -2: No "Policy Actions" In Final Communique \b0\par\par\uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1998\par 00:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  With President Bill Clinton set to visit China this week, reports that China had threatened to devaluation the yuan if the yen continued to fall - despite official protestations to the contrary - apparently convinced U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin to take action to support the yen, something he had repeatedly expressed reluctance to do. \par \par \uc2  The Tokyo meeting of deputy finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials from the Group of Seven countries and 10 of the 11 Asian countries contained in the Manila Framework was apparently called to demonstrate support for the bilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as reassure markets that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was making a concerted effort to address the disposition of massive bad loans plaguing its banks. \par \par \uc2  Reports in the Japanese press that a major policy initiative to support the yen may have been forthcoming from the meeting helped push the dollar down Friday. \par \par \uc2  But then Rubin said he wasn't expecting "policy actions" from the meeting, which was attended by Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, and the final communique proved him right. \par \par \uc2  While it contained strong language emphasizing the need for Japan to reform its banking sector and revitalize its economy, and welcomed the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. Federal Reserve and the Bank of Japan, the lack of specific policy proposals from Tokyo despite pledges to speed up the disposition of bad bank loans means that the dollar is likely to start the week well-bid. \par \par \uc2  "The promises made by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the lingering fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep the market on edge and limit the dollar's upside," said Cameron Umetsu, senior economist at LTCB Warburg Securities Ltd. in Tokyo. "But this communique doesn't provide any justification for further yen gains. A strong yen just isn't justified." \par \par \uc2  Market participants will be watching to see if Tokyo comes through with the kind of bold policies necessary to regain their trust. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said that problem loans at Japanese banks total around Y77 trillion, but many economists say the actual number is much higher. - \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 21-06-98 \par \par \uc2  2231GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6l059wr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
