\par\par \b \uc2  China urges US,  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  to keep up yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 174 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 1998\par 11:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 20 (Reuters) - The deputy governor of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Saturday that Beijing hopes the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  "We really welcome the U.S., \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was successful", Liu Mingkang told Reuters in a telephone interview. "We also hope they will continue this kind of action," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said such joint action would help to keep the yen stable. \par \par \uc2  Liu, who is in Tokyo for a meeting of senior financial officials from Asian-Pacific nations and the Group of Seven (G7) nations, said Beijing also welcomed Japan's statement of its determination to restructure its financial system and that China hoped Tokyo would carry out its efforts in a concrete and visible way. \par \par \uc2  "This is highly necessary," Liu said, adding that such restructuring would be good for Japan and regional economies. ((Tokyo Newsroom +813 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6k02fwl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
