\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-G7, Asia tell Japan to swiftly fix economy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 1998\par 05:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 20 (Reuters) - Top financial officials from the Group of Seven (G7) nations and Asia on Saturday turned up the heat on Japan to boost its flagging economy saying concrete action was "urgently needed". \par \par \uc2  The group, gathered in Tokyo to discuss Asia's year-long crisis, welcomed U.S.-Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the yen and vowed to cooperate "as appropriate" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Deputy U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers told a news conference at the end of the meeting he saw a "clear recognition" by Tokyo of the need to act fast. \par \par \uc2  But he warned that although joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had created a "window of opportunity" for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to repair its economy, the chance would not last forever. \par \par \uc2  China drove home the point, urging the United States and Japan to keep supporting the yen, whose recent slide has put pressure on Beijing to devalue its own yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - thus far a bulwark in the crisis-hit region. \par \par \uc2  "We really welcome the U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Liu Mingkang, deputy governor of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Reuters. "This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was successful. We also hope they will continue this kind of action." \par \par \uc2  Saturday's meeting came after Washington and Tokyo stunned markets on Wednesday by moving jointly to bolster the battered yen by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Their first joint yen rescue mission in more than six years was prompted by fears that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide would rob Asia of its chance to recover and trigger another round of tit-for-tat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations. \par \par \uc2  Summers had told Tokyo it must quickly forge policies to revive its economy and cure its banking woes during a flurry of crisis talks with top Japanese officials on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The theme was repeated in the final statement issued by the G7 and Japan's 11 Asian neighbours, whose clear message was Tokyo must follow words with action - and quickly. \par \par \uc2  "It is of vital importance to Japan, to the recovery of Asia, particularly those countries affected by financial market turbulence, and to the entire world economy, that Japan restore its banking system to health, achieve domestic demand-led growth, (and) open and deregulate its markets," the statement said. \par \par \uc2  The statement welcomed recent announcements on planned policy steps, but "emphasised the importance of concrete action to implement these plans quickly." \par \par \uc2  Britain's delegate told reporters Tokyo needed to set a "timetable and milestones" for measures to restore its economy. \par \par \uc2  Nigel Wicks, permanent secretary of the treasury, said if Japan set a timetable and stuck to it, Tokyo would regain the confidence and credibility of world financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The urgency of Japan's need to to sort out its banking troubles was highlighted during the meeting by news that the troubled Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan was considering a merger as one way to solve its woes. \par \par \uc2  Bank sources told Reuters a merger with a commercial bank was one option, along with independent restructuring. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara - who chaired Saturday's meeting said he thought his colleagues believed Japan was serious about reform. \par \par \uc2  Japan has enacted a 16 trillion yen ($117 billion)-plus stimulus package to kick-start its economy, which slipped into recession earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  In February, parliament passed a 30 trillion yen financial stabilisation package including 17 trillion yen to protect depositors and 13 trillion yen to recapitalise banks. \par \par \uc2  Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) will decide by July 8 on a plan to solve the banking industry's bad loan problems, LDP policy chief Taku Yamasaki was quoted by NHK television as saying on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, Summers had set Japan an ambitious three-week deadline to come up with serious policy steps and warned that Washington would keep a close tabs on the process. \par \par \uc2  But his boss, Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, noted on Friday that curing Japan's festering ills would take time. \par \par \uc2  Japan faces a key Upper House election on July 12, raising fears that the campaign could delay economic policy decisions. \par \par \uc2  The G7 groups the United States, Japan, Germany, Britain, France, Italy and Canada. They were joined by officials from the so-called Manila Group, which is made up of other nations most affected by Asia's financial turmoil. \par \par \uc2  ($1=136 yen) \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6k02c0s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
