\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis At A Glance: Mkts Await Further Action On Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 10:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Summers and Japanese Finance Minister Matsunaga agreed to be ready to continue joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a senior Finance Ministry official said. \par \par \uc2  Summers wants Japan to proceed on measures to stimulate the economy and resolve banks' bad debt problem before the July 12 election for the upper house of Parliament. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Short-term speculative players sold the dollar against the yen on expectations the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to support the yen. At 0750 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at Y135.07, down sharply from Y137.83 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Matsunaga said the recent joint action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by U.S. and Japanese monetary authorities would halt the yen's slide. Omi, director-general of Japan's Economic Planning Agency, said the yen's exchange rate with the U.S. dollar is returning to an appropriate level. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- U.S. Fed Vice Chairwoman Rivlin indicated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policies aren't constrained by efforts to support the sagging yen. \par \par \uc2  -- Most Asian stock markets fell as investors took profits and Thursday's positive yen news faded. The Philippine stock index fell 4.8%, South Korea's 3.8%. Thai stocks were down 4.3%, Indonesia's 3.2%. \par \par \uc2  -- Japanese Prime Minister Hashimoto has decided to cut corporate taxes and permanently cut income taxes, the daily Mainichi Shimbun said. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- The Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan said it's unsatisfied with Moody's downgrade of its subordinate debt rating and will seek to reverse the action. It also denied reports it would merge with Nippon Credit Bank in an effort to resolve both banks' financial problems. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- China's and Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will weather Asian turmoil, but the Japanese yen won't be so lucky, according to Credit Suisse First Boston (Hong Kong) Ltd. economists. \par \par \uc2  -- Senior G-7 central bankers won't discuss the Asian economic situation Saturday in Tokyo, officials said; the meeting of senior monetary officials from the "Manila Framework Group" will focus on stopping the yen's slide. \par \par \uc2  -- Malaysia will rely on government spending to revive its economy, with the World Bank providing some of the funding for a MYR7 billion increase in Malaysia's budget, Deputy Finance Minister Affifuddin said. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Mahathir warned that Malays would become second-class citizens if Malaysia sought IMF help, as foreigners would be able to conduct business without local involvement. [*R/MY] \par \par \uc2  -- Indonesian military chief Wiranto said the military will crack down on rumormongers thought to be fomenting national disintegration. [*R/IO] \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 19-06-98 \par \par \uc2  0837GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6j05c1l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
