\par\par \b \uc2  Yen jumps but analysts say gains may evaporate. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 22:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 19 (Reuters) - The Japanese yen gained against the dollar on Friday, still reaping the benefits of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the week, but analysts warned that the gains may evaporate unless Tokyo soon tackles economic reform. \par \par \uc2  Two days after the Federal Reserve Bank of New York and the Bank of Japan spent between $2 billion and $6 billion to stabilise the battered yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers were still scrambling to trim their dollar holdings by buying yen before the weekend. \par \par \uc2  By midafternoon in New York, the dollar had fallen nearly 3-1/2 yen from Thursday's 137.85 closing rate and traded nearly eight yen below Wednesday's pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  But the yen's gains proved frail as the dollar abruptly jumped more than a full yen as Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, in an interview with Reuters, again urged Japan to tackle its problems. At 4 p.m. EDT(200 GMT), the dollar stood at 135.83 yen. \par \par \uc2  Still, the yen's near 6 percent recovery against the dollar in three days comes nowhere close to erasing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 17 percent drop over the last year. \par \par \uc2  After Wednesday's unexpected move, which many analysts have called an about-face in U.S. policy after Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin had so long told Japan to solve its problems alone, traders Friday were in no mood to be surprised again. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing a continuation of the week's dollar sales with people expressing nervousness about the chance of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and no one is ready to take a view on the yen right here," said Earl Johnson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Montreal, in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  As talk swirled that Japan's key partners may be ready to hoist the yen even higher by forcing the dollar to trade between 125 and 130 yen, traders said attention was focused exclusively on Saturday's Group of Seven deputy finance ministers' meeting in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "I wouldn't take a gamble before we hear what the G7 have to say," said Pete Stahl, trader at ANZ Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts, however, cautioned that expectations for the weekend's hastily convened meeting may be too high. \par \par \uc2  They stressed that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was only one part of a two-pronged recovery plan for the yen and that Japan must now present a credible package of reforms. \par \par \uc2  Japan tumbled into its first recession in 23 years as the economy shrank for two consecutive quarters, and analysts said the nation's economic fundamentals look extremely gloomy. \par \par \uc2  Even as Japanese officials promised to move quickly, doubts emerged about whether Tokyo can push through permanent tax cuts and clear up its mountain of bad bank loans, often cited as the principle obstacle to economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  President Bill Clinton said he was unsure whether Japan could implement major reform before July's parliamentary elections even as Japan's Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto, in a telephone call with Clinton, had pledged to act in a "timely fashion." \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, fresh from meeting with senior Japanese policy-makers, warned that the effects of Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not last indefinitely and said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  must hurry on new economic plans. \par \par \uc2  To analysts, such scepticism suggested Japan's stabilisation programme has not been finalised. "This is not encouraging for the durability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Chris Widness, senior international economist at Chase Securities. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, eyeing a newly wobbly yen, were beginning to suggest another round of yen sales may not be far off. \par \par \uc2  Rubin said Washington was not expecting policy actions from his deputy Lawrence Summers' trip to Tokyo and said the weekend's G7 statement may include only some "boiler plate" language on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  If the meeting produces little more than a routine statement that nations still agree to cooperate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and to avoid excessively volatile movements, as Rubin's comments appeared to suggest, traders predicted yen selling would resume next week. \par \par \uc2  Added Bank of Montreal's Johnson: "Unless something comes of this meeting, and it doesn't look like it will, we could see dollar/yen move right up to 150 yen in the blink of an eye." \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar fell against the German mark, trading at 1.7887, down from Thursday's 1.7940 close. The British pound, which had benefited as investors switched funds out of yen into some European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stood at $1.6740, up from $1.6717. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded nearly unchanged against the Canadian dollar at C$1.4710 and lower at 1.4947 Swiss francs from 1.4968 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6j02o2c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
