\par\par \b \uc2  US expects quick Japan reform progress-US official. \b0\par\par\uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 12:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 19 (Reuters) - The United States expects Japan to make progress in implementing promised economic reforms in the next three weeks and will closely monitor developments in the world's number-two economy, a U.S. official said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to reporters after U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers met several top Japanese officials here, the official said the U.S. administration was convinced Tokyo is aware of the seriousness of the challenges it faces but noted the key for solving the country's problems would be a speedy and decisive implementation of reforms. \par \par \uc2  "What is crucial is the implementation of what is clearly an intent to address the problems in a strong way," the official, speaking on condition of anonymity, said. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether Washington had set a timeframe in which it expects Tokyo to act on its mounting banking sector problems and faltering domestic demand, he replied: "The senses of direction that come out in the period before the (July 12 Upper House of parliament) election will be something that will be watched very closely." \par \par \uc2  Summers earlier on Friday met Japanese Finance Minister Hirutaru Matsunaga, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Masaru Hayami and other top officials. On Saturday, he was due to attend a meeting of deputy finance ministers from the Group of Seven top industrial nations and other countries most affected by the Asian economic turmoil. \par \par \uc2  The official said there was a sense "at the highest policy level" of the importance of grappling with the economic problems besetting Japan, which slid into recession earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  Ways of improving the strength and solvency of Japan's ailing banking sector topped the agenda during Summers' meetings, the official said. \par \par \uc2  While he declined to give details of what Tokyo intends to do with the mountain of bad assets accumulated by its commercial banks, he indicated the government was moving toward a solution that would enable banks to take such assets of their balance sheets. \par \par \uc2  "There were lots of discussions here about what the best ways to do this are," he said. "We have to see just how it goes." \par \par \uc2  While noting "growing consensus on a number of important steps" required to clean up banks' balance sheets, the official warned: "There's no silver bullet here." \par \par \uc2  He added that Japanese officials appeared to recognise the importance of closing down insolvent banks rather than keeping them alive artificially by pumping in public funds. \par \par \uc2  The official also urged Tokyo to follow through on a series of promised fiscal reform measures, saying Washington's focus was on "ensuring domestic demand-led growth, which depends in part on fiscal policy". \par \par \uc2  Washington has repeatedly called for permanent tax cuts to boost consumer spending in Japan, a measure that Tokyo has so far been reluctant to commit to. \par \par \uc2  The official said this week's joint U.S.-Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to boost the sagging yen had given Tokyo "a window of opportunity" to act on its economic problems. \par \par \uc2  Just before the surprise action, the yen had fallen to its lowest level against the dollar in eight years amid mounting concern over \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic future. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the dollar down by more than six yen to about 136.80 yen in New York on Wednesday. On Friday, the dollar slipped even more and was trading at about 134.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  "There was a very good, mutually supportive tone following our joint action, the result of which was, at least so far, a source of satisfaction on both sides," he said. He added that economic policy, rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market developments, had been the prime focus of Summers' talks on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Saturday's planned meeting of G7 deputies was a "reflection of international concern about the situation in Japan", the official said. He added that Russia's economic situation was also expected to be given "some consideration" during the closed-door meeting. ((Knut Engelmann/Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8018 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6j02npa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
