\par\par \b \uc2  Stalled aid for Russia may be discussed by G7. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 09:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 19 (Reuters) - Financial aid to Russia is likely be among the issues discussed by deputy finance ministers of the Group of Seven (G7) nations when they meet in Tokyo on Saturday, analysts and officials said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Russia will be among the issues for the G7, particularly after Moscow made a plea for emergency financing," Kosuke Nakahira, a former Japanese vice finance minister for international affairs, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Russia said on Thursday that it needed at least $10 billion from the IMF to help stabilise its turbulent markets. \par \par \uc2  But the International Monetary Fund (IMF) on Thursday decided to put off the disbursement of a $670 million tranche of an existing $9.2 billion IMF loan, saying that Moscow had not fulfilled its promises on economic reform. \par \par \uc2  IMF payments to Russia have been delayed because the IMF is worried about dismal tax revenues and the degree of Moscow's commitment to its structural reform efforts. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the G7 nations backed further support for Russia through the IMF, but failed to announce a concrete aid package after a meeting of deputy finance ministers in Paris. \par \par \uc2  Asked if Japan, at the meeting, would be asked by the United States to put up fresh aid for Russia if Russia implemented substantial reform,Nakahira said: "It is possible, as they have asked many different things at one time during these sorts of occasions." \par \par \uc2  He added: "But under the prevailing rules of global society, aid to fix damage caused by short-term capital flows is centrally organised by the IMF, rather than bilaterally." \par \par \uc2  Apart from Moscow's problem, there is a problem with donors - they do not necessarily have ample funds, he said. \par \par \uc2  The United States has made clear it supported new loans, if needed, from both the IMF and the World Bank to Moscow, but has steered clear of offering bilateral aid on its own. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. administration has been asking Congress to approve $18 billion in funding for the IMF, but so far its efforts have failed. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said last month that it needed cash from its members because without further funding its usable resources would be just $10 billion to $12 billion. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said they expected to see the G7 call for Japan to buy more products made in Southeast Asia to help put the region's economies back on growth tracks, and to give new money to Moscow when it is judged eligible to receive it. \par \par \uc2  The financial stability of Russia is also important for European members of the G7, particularly for Germany, whose banking sector has Russian loan exposure of more than $30 billion, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The United States is likely to ask for future Japanese aid for Russia, either explicitly or implicitly, now that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  owes them after Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Yasushi Okada, chief economist at Credit Suisse First Boston Securities (Japan). \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan and the U.S. Federal Reserve sold an estimated $6 billion in Wednesday's joint effort to rein in the galloping dollar against the yen, traders said. The United States was estimated to have spent some $2 billion of the total, they said. \par \par \uc2  The surprise move pushed down the dollar by more than seven yen to around 136 yen in New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The two things (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japanese aid to Russia) are utterly unrelated," said an official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The official added, however, that aid to Moscow could well be on the G7 agenda. \par \par \uc2  Okada said in reference to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : "Nothing is free." \par \par \uc2  A spokesman for the Export-Import Bank of Japan said there had been no request for fresh aid from Moscow prior to the G7 deputies meeting on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  He said the bank intended to smoothly disburse $1.2 billion worth of aid and export credit earlier earmarked for Russia. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6j02nkd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
