\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Summers urges quick Japan action on economy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Eiichiro Tokumoto \par \uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 11:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 19 (Reuters) - U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, in a flurry of talks here on Friday with Japanese officials, said Japan must act fast to revive its economy before the impact of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  faded. \par \par \uc2  Two days after the sometimes feuding allies joined forces to prop up the ailing yen, Summers held a series of high-level meetings urging Tokyo to stabilise its financial system and get its economy back on a recovery track. \par \par \uc2  Former prime minister Kiichi Miyazawa, who heads a ruling party government panel on the bad loan problem at Japanese banks, said Summers told him during their meeting that Japan must act on both issues before the effect of Wednesday's surprise yen rescue \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  waned. \par \par \uc2  "A point Mr Summers stressed was that Japan should take clear action on the bad loan problems and on economic recovery before the impact of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fades," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Summers also said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should abandon its "convoy" system - in which stronger financial firms and the government rescued weaker institutions, however poorly run - and urged Tokyo to make clear how it intended to clean up its bad loan problems, Miyazawa said. \par \par \uc2  Summers also urged Japan to enact permanent cuts in personal income tax rates, a step many economists favour but which is a politically tricky measure to take, Miyazawa said. \par \par \uc2  Summers stressed the "act fast" theme earlier in the day, telling Foreign Minister Keizo Obuchi he was happy with the effects of the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  must move fast on financial stabilisation, a Foreign Ministry official said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the first joint yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than six years, took place after the yen's fall to eight-year lows of around 146 to the dollar sparked fears of an Asian economic meltdown. The greenback was trading at just above 135 yen late on Friday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The mountain of bad loans held by banks, left over from the collapsed "bubble" economy of the 1980s and aggravated by nearly eight years of sluggish growth and falling asset prices, is one of Japan's most pressing problems. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto said the government would promote the disposal of such loans, especially those with land as collateral, through steps such as asset securitisation. \par \par \uc2  He offered no concrete plans but a special group within the ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) is looking at ways to encourage banks to get the bad loans off their books. \par \par \uc2  Also on Friday, LDP Secretary-General Koichi Kato said he wanted a quick decision from the party on one idea under consideration, the creation of a "bridge bank" which would provide loans to sound borrowers left in the lurch by bank failures, Jiji news agency reported. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets are keen to see whether the Group of Seven (G7) industrial nations will throw its support behind the U.S.-Japan rescue effort for the yen at a hastily arranged meeting of its deputy finance ministers in Tokyo on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  The meeting will bring together deputy finance ministers from the G7 as well as finance officials from Asia. \par \par \uc2  "As U.S. President (Bill) Clinton and Prime Minister Hashimoto have recognised, the U.S. and Japan share a very strong interest in a return to sustainable growth on the Asian continent," Summers told reporters on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara said on Friday the nation's concern over excessive weakness in the yen had not changed. \par \par \uc2  Summers met Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga later on Friday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the two discussed taxes but that Summers made no specific proposals. \par \par \uc2  The United States has been strongly urging Japan to make permanent income tax cuts to help spur domestic economic demand but Japanese officials have been reluctant, insisting cuts should be part of broader tax reform. \par \par \uc2  The yen's relentless fall against the dollar had prompted calls for action from the rest of the world to halt the slide that threatened to touch off another round of Asia's economic crisis by triggering round-robin \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations. \par \par \uc2  The sharpest warning came from China on Wednesday, ahead of the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when Vice Minister for Foreign Trade Sun Zhenyu told Reuters that China may have to consider making "adjustments" in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the weak yen continued to pressure its trade and exports. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8018 tokyo.newsroomzreuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6j02ndv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
