\par\par \b \uc2  Yen Cross-Mark stays weak on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  caution. \b0\par\par\uc2 502 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 08:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 19 (Reuters) - The mark remained weak against the yen by mid-afternoon trade on Friday in Tokyo cross market as caution over possible yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen mainly followed movements of the dollar/yen trade, and the market was nervous about selling the Japanese unit aggressively in the cross market, they said. \par \par \uc2  Undertone of the German unit has been slightly bearish as the delay of an international loan to Russia raised speculation of further financial instability there, which could put selling pressure on the mark over the longer term, they said. \par \par \uc2  The market's reluctance to sell yen actively ahead of the Group of Seven (G7) deputies meeting in Tokyo on Saturday also weighed on mark/yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "After the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (on Wednesday), operators became more careful about selling the yen actively in the cross market as well," a senior foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar fell to 130 yen, then mark/yen could edge down to around 73.50 yen, but there should be good buying demand at that level," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  At 0600 GMT, the mark stood at 76.32 yen compared with 76.84 in late New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A series of comments by Japanese officials and by U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers had no major impact on the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A senior official of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  told reporters on Friday afternoon that Japan and the U.S. were ready to continue cooperation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The official was briefing reporters after a meeting in Tokyo between Japan's Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga and U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers. \par \par \uc2  "We are prepared to continue to cooperate in the market as needed," the MOF official said. \par \par \uc2  Japan and the United States will also continue to cooperate in stabilising Asian financial markets and economies, he said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Kyodo news agency reported on Friday, quoting unidentified sources, that Japan's government was mulling steps to help reconstruct ailing Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan Ltd (LTCB)\tab    , including a possible merger with Nippon Credit Bank (NCB)\tab    .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The report was later denied by both banks and by Japanese monetary officials.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another foreign bank dealer, however, said that such a move could be a positive factor for the yen because restructuring within the banking sector could result in improvement of the Japanese financial system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The yen was briefly put under selling pressure after the report, but it was bought back shortly," he said. "Wasn't this what the market was looking for? Obviously a stronger Japanese financial system would be bullish news for the yen," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As of 0600 GMT, the British pound was at 228.66/78 yen compared with 230.44 yen in late New York on Thursday. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6j02n9h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
