\par\par \b \uc2  Summers urges quick Japan action on economy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Teruaki Ueno \par \uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 07:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 19 (Reuters) - U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers told Tokyo on Friday that while Washington was happy with the effects of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  must now act quickly to boost its economy. \par \par \uc2  "It is important that measures already announced be quickly implemented," a Japanese Foreign Ministry official quoted Summers as telling Foreign Minister Keizo Obuchi in a meeting in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Summers also told Obuchi he was happy with the effects of the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was carried on Wednesday night and brought the yen back from eight-year lows against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But Japan must act quickly to stabilise its financial system, Summers said. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan (BOJ) Governor Masaru Hayami told Summers that it was important for Japan to solve its bad loan problem quickly. \par \par \uc2  "Governor Hayami emphasised the need for Japan to quickly solve its bad loan problem, and the U.S. side expressed their understanding," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  The mountains of problems loans at Japan's banks left over from the collapsed "bubble" economy of the 1980s is one of the most pressing problems afflicting Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto said Japan's government would promote the disposal of such loans, especially those with land as collateral, through measures such as asset securitisation. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets were nervous on Friday over whether the Group of Seven (G7) would throw its support behind the U.S.-Japan rescue effort for the yen at a hastily-arranged meeting of its deputy finance ministers in Tokyo on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Summers, who arrived on Thursday evening, met over dinner with Japan's top financial diplomat, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara, the head of Saturday's meeting, which will also include finance officials from Asia. \par \par \uc2  The business newspaper Nihon Keizai Shimbun, in an unsourced article, said the two men agreed at the unofficial meeting to continue bilateral cooperation towards correcting the yen's weakness and stabilising Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "As U.S. President (Bill) Clinton and Prime Minister Hashimoto have recognised, the U.S. and Japan share a very strong interest in a return to sustainable growth on the Asian continent," Summers told reporters on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said on Friday that Japan's concern over excessive weakness in the yen had not changed. \par \par \uc2  Summers met Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga later on Friday and was due to meet two top officials of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party, Taku Yamasaki and Koichi Kato, as well as former prime minister Kiichi Miyazawa in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was down on its Thursday New York closing levels in early afternoon Tokyo. It was trading at around 136.90 yen, against 137.80/90, as nervousness spread through the market ahead of the meetings. \par \par \uc2  The two economic superpowers jolted markets on Wednesday by conducting their first joint yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than six years, sending the dollar sharply lower against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen's relentless fall against the dollar had prompted calls for action from the rest of the world to halt the slide that threatened to touch off another round of Asia's economic crisis by triggering round-robin \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations. \par \par \uc2  The sharpest warning came from China on Wednesday, ahead of the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when Vice Minister for Foreign Trade Sun Zhenyu told Reuters that China may have to consider making "adjustments" in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the weak yen continued to pressure its trade and exports. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8018 tokyo.newsroomzreuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6j02n0p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
