\par\par \b \uc2  Asian Crisis At A Glance: BOJ Doesn't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2 , But Yen Up \b0\par\par\uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 10:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- Traders watched nervously in Tokyo, but there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action Thursday to prop up the yen after massive joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday by the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At 0800 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at Y136.90, down from Y143.33 in Tokyo late Wednesday, before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The joint move came in the context of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's commitment to economic reforms, U.S. Treasury Secretary Rubin said. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose sharply in line with the yen's rally, but stabilized as dollar-buying interest emerged at new levels. The Indonesian rupiah remained very weak with poor sentiment, trading at IDR15,700 at 0745 GMT. \par \par \uc2  -- Tokyo stocks rallied on the yen's surge; the Nikkei rose more than 600 points to end 4.4% higher. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  Other Asian stock indexes soared on news of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The South Korean exchange led the recovery with a 7.1% rise; Malaysia's rose 6.8%; the Philippines' rose 6.5%, Hong Kong's rose 6.4% and Thailand's was up 6.9%. \par \par \uc2  -- South Korea's Financial Supervisory Commission unveiled 55 companies on the "black list" of companies deemed nonviable, including 20 belonging to Korea's five largest conglomerates - Hyundai, Samsung, Daewoo, LG and SK. [*R/SK] \par \par \uc2  -- China Vice Minister of Foreign Trade Sun refuted a report that China would consider devaluing the yuan if the weakening yen caused its exports to plunge, official media said. Foreign Ministry spokesman Zhu said he welcomed the stronger yen and reaffirmed China's commitment not to devalue the yuan, calling that stance China's contribution to financial stability in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Adding value to exports and restructuring businesses are the solution to a strong yuan, not devaluation, Chinese trading company executives said. [*R/CH] \par \par \uc2  -- The Indonesian government and Japan's Export-Import Bank signed a $1 billion loan to boost Indonesia's exports, part of Japan's emergency economic package to stabilize Southeast Asian economies. [*R/IO] [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- The Philippines may be the only Southeast Asian country to post GDP growth in 1998, the U.S.-based Institute of International Finance said. [*R/RP] \par \par \uc2  -- Japan's government and Liberal Democratic Party may announce permanent income tax cuts prior to the upper house elections July 12, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Japan posted a merchandise trade surplus of Y1.219 trillion in May, up 67% from May 1997 and the 14th straight month the trade surplus has risen on year. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Malaysia's Finance Minister and Deputy Prime Minister Anwar followed the party line in blaming the ringgit's plunge on irrational market forces, and called for an overhaul of the global financial system. [*R/MY] \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 18-06-98 \par \par \uc2  0842GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6i05c36\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
