\par\par \b \uc2  G-7 Central Bankers Don't Plan To Attend Tokyo Meeting \b0\par\par\uc2  By David G. Pearson \par \uc2 321 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 20:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  PARIS (Dow Jones)--Senior central bankers from the Group of Seven (G-7) countries don't plan to travel to Tokyo this weekend for a meeting to discuss the critical economic and monetary situation in Japan and Asia, officials said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The meeting of senior monetary officials from the "Manila Framework Group" of 22 nations was called hastily as part of a U.S.-led strategy to arrest the slide of the yen against the dollar that threatened to plunge Asia into a new financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Representatives from the International Monetary Fund, the World Bank and the Asian Development Bank will also attend the talks on Saturday alongside Treasury officials from the big industrial countries and some from Asia and Latin America. \par \par \uc2  Normally, the so-called G-7 deputies - second-level officials from finance ministries and central banks in the U.S., Japan, Germany, France, Italy, the U.K. and Canada - get together three or four times a year to discuss economic and monetary policy issues. \par \par \uc2  Occasionally, however, the central bankers and Treasury officials meet separately. \par \par \uc2  Officials said there's less need for a full deputies' meeting because they met only last week in Paris. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials at the emergency gathering might be construed by the market as being somewhat negative for the yen. \par \par \uc2  They argue that it may give a signal that the G-7 countries aren't preparing a strategy for concerted yen-support \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  following Wednesday's successful joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Federal Reserve to prop up the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Avinash Persaud, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst for J.P. Morgan in London, says he sees only a "modest" risk of renewed downward pressure on the yen in the short-term because of that perception. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 18-06-98 \par \par \uc2  1818GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6i058n2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
