\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar/yen steady in Europe, eyes G7's next move. \b0\par\par\uc2 490 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 18 (Reuters) - The dollar was calm in relatively narrow ranges against the yen at midday Europe, but remained wary of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support for the yen and the outcome a weekend G7 deputies' meeting. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers' Tokyo visit today and Friday ahead of the G7 compounded the caution in a market still dazed from Wednesday's U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market will be looking for something out of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  this weekend. It looks like the U.S. will expect something in return for its cooperation on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Claudio Piron, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  By 1050 GMT, the dollar was trading at 136.79/89 yen, above the overnight low of 135.50 yen but still stuck in the 136.30-137.30 range that has persisted for the European session. \par \par \uc2  But this was still well below levels around 142.00 yen seen before Wednesday's joint Federal Reserve and Bank of Japan dollar/yen sales, and a far cry from Tuesday's near eight-year high of 146.75 yen. \par \par \uc2  Summers meets Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami and Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga on Friday. Summers said U.S. and Japan economic policies and Asian problems will be discussed. \par \par \uc2  "For now, the market would be sensible to give the authorities the benefit of the doubt, in which case I doubt whether we will see any decent bounce in dollar/yen over the next few days," said Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  However, markets still remain wary that this round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have done little to improve \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gloomy economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  "We still need to see more tax cuts and economic reforms implemented in Japan before the weak yen trend will change," said James McKay, economist at Commonwealth Bank of Australia. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark, meantime, was largely steady, trading at 1.7855/60 from 1.7887/92 marks. \par \par \uc2  Germany's IFO May business climate survey for West Germany came in at 98.7, unchanged from April and in line with expectations. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank member Reimut Jochimsen said today Germany's GDP growth in 1998 will likely succeed 1997's 2.2 percent and said German domestic demand had increased significantly since the beginning of the year. \par \par \uc2  His comments echoed the earlier assessment of the German economy in the German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monthly report for June. \par \par \uc2  "The economic outlook is improving in Germany and rate hike expectations are also growing," said a dealer at a French bank. \par \par \uc2  "The Bundesbank says it will be considering conditions across Europe for its monetary policy decisions, but this doesn't mean we'll have to wait until the third or fourth quarter for them to move." \par \par \uc2  ((Andrew Reierson, London newsroom +44 171 542 6745, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02rw5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
