\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr down but off low, jittery before Summers visit. \b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 08:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  0600 GMT\tab     TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0600 GMT\par  USD/JPY     136.37/40\tab \tab 137.08-135.50\par  USD/DEM     1.7847/49\tab \tab 1.7873-1.7810\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2      TOKYO, June 18 (Reuters) - The dollar was down but off its \par \par \uc2  earlier low against the yen by late afternoon in Tokyo on \par \par \uc2  Thursday, but the greenback was seen vulnerable due to a \par \par \uc2  possibility of more yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks. \par \par \uc2  The market was also nervous ahead of a Japan visit by U.S. \par \par \uc2  Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers and a weekend \par \par \uc2  meeting of G7 deputies in Tokyo, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the dollar slipped as low as 135.50 yen on stop-loss dollar selling and fears that the Bank of Japan (BOJ) might continue to step in and sell dollars after the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  But the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not been detected in the market so far on Thursday in Tokyo, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The greenback quickly pared losses on short-covering by interbank players and buying by Japanese institutional investors and importers, after earlier downside tests were blocked by solid bids by U.S. fund operators around 135.50 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded narrowly above 136 yen in the afternoon, but activity was brisker than price moves suggested. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar/yen was range-bound as Japanese investors and overseas fund operators were detected trading actively on both bid and offer sides," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  "But the equilibrium may break after a series of important meetings that start later today," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  A senior official of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  told reporters that Summers was likely to hold talks with Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara in Tokyo later on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Summers will also meet Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga from 11.50 a.m. (0250 GMT) on Friday, he said. \par \par \uc2  Summers will attend a meeting in Tokyo on Saturday of deputy finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials from the G7 countries and from nations that take part in the Manila framework, the MOF official said. \par \par \uc2  He said the nations were likely to issue some sort of a statement after the meeting. \par \par \uc2  In the near term, the dollar's fate may depend on whether support holds around 135 yen, where knock-out options are rumoured to be placed, said a Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the longer-term dollar/yen outlook was mixed. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors who believe the yen's weak trend is unchanged are willing to bargain-hunt the dollar," said a dealer at a trust bank. "But those who fear a further dollar drop are looking to sell for hedging purposes." \par \par \uc2  But dealers agreed that the risk was greater on the downside in the near future. \par \par \uc2  "Many operators are expecting the BOJ to step in if the dollar rises rapidly. In this mood, bidders were very careful and speculators were quickly taking profits even at minor rallies," a foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve and the BOJ jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight and were estimated to have spent as much as $6 billion selling dollars for yen in New York overnight. \par \par \uc2  The surprising joint effort by the U.S. and Japan hammered down the dollar by more than six yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto said on Thursday he was happy with the impact the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States had on the market. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto told reporters: "I am happy with the fact that the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was appreciated by the market. But I don't want to comment on (specific \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) levels." \par \par \uc2  But a dealer at a city bank said: "Unless the Hashimoto government really takes bolder steps to stimulate the economy, the yen will just resume its downtrend." \par \par \uc2  The market was unmoved by Japan's customs-cleared trade data for May, which showed a 66.6 percent rise in surplus from a year earlier to 1.22 trillion yen. Economists had forecast, on average, a surplus of 1.15 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 136.90/00 yen and 1.7870/80 marks in New York on Wednesday. ((Kanta Watanabe/Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02rrt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
