\par\par \b \uc2  China says Japan-U.S. cooperation on yen necessary. \b0\par\par\uc2 303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 09:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, June 18 (Reuters) - Japan and the United States must take a common stance toward maintaining a strong yen, China's foreign ministry spokesman said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Spokesman Zhu Bangzao said China welcomed efforts by Washington and Tokyo to reach a "common understanding" on the yen and move to halt its slide. \par \par \uc2  "This was necessary and we welcome it," Zhu told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  "China hopes the relevant countries fulfil their obligation and responsibilities to appropriately tackle problems and implement this common understanding and make continuing efforts to promote stability and development in Asia," he said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong hailed the overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States to bolster the yen, and said a devaluation of the Chinese yuan was not necessary. \par \par \uc2  "We are glad to see that the exchange rate of the Japanese yen is picking up," Xinhua quoted Dai as telling a gathering of Asian bankers in Beijing. \par \par \uc2  Dai, governor of the People's Bank of China, also reaffirmed that China would not devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It's unnecessary to do that," he was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday the battered yen surged after U.S.-Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, triggering rallies in stock markets across the United States and Europe. \par \par \uc2  Asian markets followed suit on Thursday, opening sharply higher as relief spread to the beleaguered region. \par \par \uc2  An economist at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the yen's rebound was "good news for China". \par \par \uc2  Beijing had since last week steadily turned up the heat on Japan and the United States in its efforts to force them to halt the yen's precipitous slide. \par \par \uc2  - Beijing Newsroom (86) 10-6532-1921; Fax (86) 10-6532-4978 \par \par \uc2  - Email: beijing.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02rca\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
