\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-China jawbones its way to higher profile. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Eckert \par \uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, June 18 (Reuters) - China deftly mixed veiled devaluation threats and appeals for "courage and wisdom" to prod the United States and Japan to halt the yen's alarming slide, analysts said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Turning weakness into strength in the best tradition of ancient Chinese military strategists, Beijing played on Washington's worst nightmare: a devaluation of the yuan that would spark a dangerous new phase in the Asian financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "They did get the U.S. attention, they did get U.S. action," said Bill Belchere, head of fixed income and economic research at Merrill Lynch in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "And from that vantage point, perhaps China did a great service for Asia in giving it some breathing room." \par \par \uc2  In a surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the New York Federal Reserve moved to boost the yen, by some estimates spending up to $6 billion. The yen's rally lifted stock markets worldwide, giving Asia its first good news in weeks. \par \par \uc2  Beyond winning relief for Asia, China also jawboned its way to a bigger role in international financial affairs. \par \par \uc2  "This is the first time China has seriously talked about another country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said one Shanghai-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. \par \par \uc2  "The Chinese are trying to strengthen their image in the world, especially Asia," he said. "They want to show China is one of the leaders in the economic world." \par \par \uc2  As the yen continued its slide past the 140 yen/dollar barrier, Chinese officials turned up the heat, warning of pressures on the yuan as a result of the yen's weakness. \par \par \uc2  Dai Xianglong, governor of the People's Bank of China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , started the drumbeat last weak, sparking jitters about a yuan devaluation by saying the yen's drop was having a severe impact on China's trade and investment. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday Finance Minister Xiang Huaicheng warned that the yuan could be undermined if China missed its eight percent growth target this year. Growth fell short of that mark in the first quarter of this year, reaching 7.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  China's official spokesman chimed in, urging the United States and Japan to "muster the courage and wisdom to face reality and take effective measures to stop the yen's slide". \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday Sun Zhenyu, the vice minister for foreign trade, issued the most explicit warning yet. \par \par \uc2  "If the change in the exchange rate puts large pressure on foreign trade and exports, or if there is a large fall in foreign trade and exports, then I am afraid we may have to consider the question of whether to make adjustments," he told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Although Sun denied making the comments, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worked its magic, raising the question of whether he misspoke or spoke with tactical brilliance. \par \par \uc2  The upshot is that China got its way, drawing the United States in from the sidelines after Japanese pleas had failed to move U.S. officials frustrated at Tokyo's inaction on its economic problems. \par \par \uc2  "There's probably a sense of frustration among the Chinese officials that while they have been asked to do the right thing and they have done it, Japan hasn't been seen to do its part," said Chiang Yao Chye, head of Asia Pacific research at CIBC in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "They are signalling to the U.S. that if it comes to the crunch and the yen weakens further and should they have to devalue it's not their fault," he said. \par \par \uc2  Chiang said China's wielding of new-found financial clout pointed to the reality of a future Chinese economic power "even though they are nowhere near the sort of per-capita income that we see in the other more developed countries in Asia". \par \par \uc2  "When they finally get there China is going to be very big and the U.S. can't afford to ignore that," he said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02lk3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
