\par\par \b \uc2  =Analysts Voice Caution About Dlr-Yen Effect On U.S. Tsys \b0\par\par\uc2  By Guy Dixon and John Montgomery \par \uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 20:53\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The rug was suddenly yanked out\par Wednesday from under the Treasurys market, after having\par benefited for weeks from a soaring dollar and a safe-haven\par bid.\par    But while the downdraft in Treasurys appeared severe\par this session, many strategists say it may be a passing phase\par rather than a reversal in the market's fortunes.\par    The abrupt turnaround came after joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Federal Reserve and Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par support the yen early Wednesday sent Treasury prices\par reeling. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  action, which began just as New\par York financial markets were opening, sent the dollar\par plunging to around Y136.25 by early afternoon Wednesday from\par Y143.33 late Tuesday in New York.\par    Treasurys have bounced slightly from their early lows,\par and market watchers say this session's events may be a\par temporary plot twist, rather than a permanent turning point\par in the Asian drama and the safe-haven supporting role for\par U.S. Treasurys.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    "A lot depends on how well this \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sticks,"\par said William Kan, a Treasury market analyst at Merrill Lynch\par in New York. The question remains whether one morning's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is enough to undo the mounting doubt about\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's intentions to implement reforms.\par    The yield on the Treasury's 30-year long bond has backed\par up to around 5.73% from 5.65% on Tuesday and an all-time low\par of 5.58% on Monday. But although the market has retracted,\par the long bond's yield remains below 5.75% - still seen as a\par key psychological barrier.\par    Meanwhile, despite pledges by Japan's Finance Minister\par Hikaru Matsunaga to spur domestic demand and take all steps\par necessary to rebuild the Japanese banking system, investors\par may continue to wait out any rounds of Japanese reforms by\par staying parked in U.S. Treasurys.\par    "A lot needs to be done over the next several days," Kan\par added. "I wouldn't be surprised if some (safe-haven buying)\par came back into Treasurys," he said, noting that "the crisis\par in Asia is still looming over the market."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  06-17-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1453EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4231 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du6h01jf0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
