\par\par \b \uc2  Yen's future hinges on Tokyo's economic moves, analysts say. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 20:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Reuters) - The yen jumped against the dollar Wednesday as central banks spent billions to prop up the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but analysts said the yen's future hinges on Tokyo's ability to offer a credible plan to boost Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  Joining forces shortly before the official start of U.S. trading, the Federal Reserve and the Bank of Japan spent an estimated $6 billion to send the dollar plunging over four yen to its 136.05 yen session low. \par \par \uc2  "The move certainly surprised the market, even though participants had been speculating about when and how the central banks might show their hand," said Ben Restivo, assistant vice president at Bank Brussels Lambert in New York. \par \par \uc2  While traders called the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  a success, admitting to nervousness about more possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action before Asian markets open Thursday, analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone would not reverse the yen's year-long decline. \par \par \uc2  Echoing the Clinton administration's recent warnings that Japan needs to concentrate on correcting fundamental problems to rekindle domestic demand-led economic growth, they said investors would watch Tokyo's next steps very carefully. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe the market is holding back now and this move could help in the very short term. But if the economic fundamentals aren't there, the dollar is going right back up," warned Christine Schneider, associate economist at Donaldson, Lufkin & Jenrette Securities Corp. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts expressed concern the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was useless given the free-market theory that markets will find their own levels regardless of government interference. \par \par \uc2  "To think this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to have a lasting effect without some major medicine being taken by Japan is preposterous," said Dick Dahlberg, who oversees $6 billion of money for Salomon Brothers Asset Management. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, confirming that the government had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said it took place "in the context of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plans to strengthen its economy" and said the United States was ready to continue the selling if needed. \par \par \uc2  Japan tumbled into its first recession in 23 years as the economy shrank for two consecutive quarters, and analysts said the country's economic fundamentals looked extremely gloomy. \par \par \uc2  Even a positive effect on exports from the yen's 12-percent drop against the dollar this year was not enough to hint that recovery may be on the way, economists said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they would wait to gauge the effect Wednesday's dollar sales had on Asian markets and for news about hastily arranged meetings between U.S. and Japanese officials. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers and William McDonough, president of the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, which conducts the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , were expected to arrive in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street money managers said they needed to see some serious buckling down by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effect would be only temporary. Stock analysts and traders have said Japan needs to write off bad bank loans and be more aggressive in stimulating its economy. \par \par \uc2  "It is very unlikely the Clinton administration would have agreed to sell dollars unless Japan had made big promises," said Henry Willmore, senior economist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  Concessions may have been made for permanent tax cuts and more efforts to clean up the ailing banking sector, economists said. But after a string of rescue packages that at first dazzled but ultimately disappointed investors, analysts said the market must now be shown something new. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe Japan will give further assurances on tax cuts but they really have to indicate that they mean it this time for markets to believe them," said Donaldson, Lufkin's Schneider. \par \par \uc2  Any new round of yen selling may be a few days off, dealers said, noting continuing concerns about when or if Japan's other Group of Seven partners, who sat out Wednesday's dollar sales, may strike. \par \par \uc2  "The Fed's sales may be the first blow and the G7, like Germany, may want to keep its powder dry for another day," Willmore said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02shh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
