\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-U.S. rides to rescue of ailing Japanese yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 18:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, June 17 (Reuters) - The United States rode to the rescue of the battered Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday in a drastic shift from its recent hands-off approach amid mounting concern over Japan's slumping economy. \par \par \uc2  Joining forces with Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Treasury Department instructed the New York Federal Reserve to spend an estimated $2 billion to buy the yen in a bid to drive up its value against the dollar - the first U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the yen in more than six years. \par \par \uc2  The yen, which recently has fallen to its lowest level in eight years as concerns spread about Japan's sick economy, immediately strengthened against the dollar and gained further ground after Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said Washington stood ready to continue the action if needed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to 137 yen immediately after the surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from 142 yen just before. \par \par \uc2  News of the action hit nervous \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets only hours before Rubin's deputy Lawrence Summers was due to leave for Tokyo to meet senior Japanese officials for hastily convened crisis talks on the economic situation in Japan and elsewhere in troubled Asia. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, he was also due to attend a meeting of deputy finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrial nations. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared limited to U.S. and Japanese authorities, without a hand from other big industrialised countries. Britain said the action was a U.S.-Japanese initiative and wished it every success. \par \par \uc2  "This is a clear shift in U.S. policy and it probably indicates that the U.S. has received some concessions from Japan," said Henry Willmore, senior economist at Barclays Capital in New York. \par \par \uc2  In a statement, Rubin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place "in the context of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plans to strengthen its economy." \par \par \uc2  Washington has repeatedly called on Tokyo to do more to stimulate demand and Treasury has been increasingly frustrated with Japan's reluctance to commit to specific plans. \par \par \uc2  The White House said the rare \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had come at a key moment for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the world's number two economy. \par \par \uc2  "The president (Bill Clinton) - and the United States government - obviously believes that this is an important moment for Japan to deal with the challenges that it faces," spokesman Mike McCurry told a news briefing. \par \par \uc2  The sagging yen and the decline of Japan's economy, which has entered a recession, have put additional economic pressure on already struggling nations across the Asian continent. \par \par \uc2  Exporters in countries such as South Korea, Indonesia or Thailand - at the receiving end of multibillion dollar bailout deals - need the Japanese market to sell their products, but are unable to do so since the weak yen makes their products more expensive for the Japanese to buy. \par \par \uc2  Behind the scenes, U.S. officials have expressed concern the yen's weakness may set off a new round of devaluations across Asia, and particularly in China, the world's most populous nation. \par \par \uc2  "The risk of a spreading of the crisis and a deepening of the crisis was so immediate," said Fred Bergsten, head of the Institute for International Economics. "If the yen continued to fall the risk that there would be a whole new round of competitive devaluations was very acute." \par \par \uc2  McCurry suggested the yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have reduced the pressure on Beijing to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan. Asked about reports China had felt renewed pressures to devalue, he told a briefing "that may have been before some of the things that we've been discussing in the last few minutes." \par \par \uc2  "The president has noted approvingly the attitude Chinese authorities have taken with respect to Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. Clinton joined by Rubin and other top U.S. officials are due to visit China next week. \par \par \uc2  Clinton and Japan's Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto talked about the Japanese economy by telephone on Tuesday night, just hours before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  began. \par \par \uc2  Rubin suggested the United States now expected \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to carry out stringent economic reforms to stimulate demand, strengthen its banking system and open markets, which he has said are the only long-term remedies to bolster the yen's value. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02s38\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
