\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar capped vs yen in Tokyo afternoon. \b0\par\par\uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 07:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      TOKYO, June 17 (Reuters) - ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACTIVITY: \par \par \uc2      0518 GMT -- *Dollar capped against yen in Tokyo afternoon \par \par \uc2  and under slight downward pressure as market turned cautious \par \par \uc2  about buying dollars aggressively. \par \par \uc2      *Speculators unwinding longs ahead of possible visit to \par \par \uc2  Japan by U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Summers expected on \par \par \uc2  Thursday. \par \par \uc2      *"The market is turning a bit cautious about extending bids \par \par \uc2  above 144 yen before the visit of Summers," a dealer at a \par \par \uc2  foreign bank said. "Further position adjustments may push down \par \par \uc2  the dollar below 143 yen in the short term." \par \par \uc2  *Dollar at 143.30/40 yen against 143.62/64 at Tokyo midday. \par \par \uc2  *A Japanese government source said on Wednesday Summers is likely to visit Japan from Thursday. Asked about the purpose of the visit, the source, who asked not to be identified, told Reuters: "I think his coming means he has a message he would like to deliver to our side." \par \par \uc2  *A Finance Ministry official told Reuters that Japan's top financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara will likely learn later on Wednesday whether he will keep his job as vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  0300 GMT \par \par \uc2  *Dollar capped below 144 yen by midday due to sporadic sales by exporters and caution ahead of U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers' expected visit to Japan this week. \par \par \uc2  *Weak sentiment for dollar that prevailed in early trade erased by brisk bargain-hunting by investors, falls in Indonesia rupiah and loss of gains in Tokyo stocks. \par \par \uc2  *"The news of Summers' visit to Japan has served as a deterrent for the dollar's rise," said a senior dealer at a U.S. bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  *Haruhiko Kuroda, head of International Finance Bureau of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told reporters he had heard Summers would visit Japan, but that he did not know the schedule or expected topics of discussion. \par \par \uc2  *The Japanese business daily Nihon Keizai Shimbun quoted U.S. government sources as saying Summers plans to have emergency meetings on Thursday with Japanese officials to discuss the yen's fall and shaky Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  *U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said on Tuesday a decision on whether to send his deputy to Tokyo would be made shortly. News of Summers' trip to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised speculation over concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt yen's fall, but traders here were mixed about the main purpose of his visit. \par \par \uc2  *"Discussions about concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be made over the phone," said a senior trader at a long-term credit bank. \par \par \uc2  *"I think that the main purpose of his visit is to make Japan aware of the severity of Japan's economic situation and to urge it to take drastic steps (on its economy and bad loans)," he added. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02qo3\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\l